\id 1CH - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.23
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Chronicles
\toc1 The first Sequential Account
\toc2 1 Chronicles
\mt2 The first
\mt1 Sequential Account
\mt3 commonly called
\mt2 1 Chronicles
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Adam’s descendants
\r (Gen. 5:1-32; 10:1-32; 11:10-26)
\p
\v 1 Adam, Seth, Enosh,
\v 2 Keynan, Mahalalel, Jared,
\v 3 Enoch, Methuselah, Lamech,
\v 4 Noah, Shem, Ham, and Japheth.
\p
\v 5 Japheth’s sons were Gomer, Magog, Maday, Yavan, Tuval, Meshek, and Tiras.
\v 6 Gomer’s sons were Ashkenaz, Rifat, and Togarmah.
\v 7 Yavan’s sons were Elishah, Tarshish, Kitim, and Dodanim.
\p
\v 8 Ham’s sons were Kush, Mitsrayim, Put, and Canaan.
\v 9 Kush’s sons were Seva, Havilah, Savta, Raemah, and Savteka. Raemah’s sons were Sheba and Dedan.
\v 10 Then Kush fathered Nimrod \add and\add* he became a mighty \add warrior\add*.
\v 11 Mitsrayim was the ancestor of the Lud, the Anamim, the Lehavim, the Naftuhim,
\v 12 the Patrusim, the Kasluhim, and the Kaftor people groups. (The Philistines descended from the Kasluhim people group.)
\v 13 Canaan’s first son was Sidon, and then he was also the ancestor of the Hittites,
\v 14 the Jebusites, the Amorites, the Girgashim,
\v 15 the Hivim, the Arkim, the Sinim,
\v 16 the Arvadim, the Tsemarim, and the Hamatim.
\p
\v 17 Shem’s sons were Eylam, Ashur, Arphaxad, Lud, and Aram. \add Aram’s sons were\add*: Uz, Hul, Geter, and Meshek.
\v 18 Arphaxad was the father of Shelah, and Shelah was the father of Eber.
\v 19 Eber had two sons: Peleg (\add which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘divided’\add*, because in his lifetime, the earth was divided) and Yaketan.
\v 20 Yaketan was the ancestor of: Almodad, Shelef, Hatsarmavet, Yerah,
\v 21 Hadoram, Uzal, Diklah,
\v 22 Eyval, Avimael, Sheba,
\v 23 Ofir, Havilah, and Yovav. All those were the descendants of Yaketan.
\p
\v 24 \add These were the descendants of\add* Shem \add (in order from him to Abraham\add* \add )\add*: Arphaxad, Shelah,
\v 25 Eber, Peleg, Reu,
\v 26 Serug, Nahor, Terah,
\v 27 and Abram (\add whose name God later changed to\add* Abraham).
\s1 Ishmael’s descendants
\r (Gen. 25:12-16)
\p
\v 28 Abraham’s sons were Isaac and Ishmael.
\v 29 These were their family lines: The first son born to Ishmael \add (the son of Abraham’s slave-wife Hagar)\add* was Nevayot, then his other sons Kedar, Adbe’el, Mivsam,
\v 30 Mishma, Dumah, Masa, Hadad, Teyma,
\v 31 Yetur, Nafish, and Kedemah. Those were Ishmael’s \add twelve\add* sons.
\v 32 The sons of Abraham’s slave-wife Keturah were Zimran, Yakeshan, Medan, Midian, Yishbak, and Shuah. Yakeshan’s sons were Sheba and Dedan.
\v 33 Midian’s sons were Eyfah, Efer, Hanok, Avida, and Elda’ah. All those were Keturah’s descendants.
\p
\v 34 Abraham had his son Isaac \add by his wife Sarah\add*, and Isaac’s sons were Esau and \add Jacob\add*\add , whose name God later changed to\add* Israel.
\s1 Esau’s descendants
\r (Gen. 36:1-19)
\p
\v 35 Esau’s sons were Elifaz, Re’uel, Ye’ush, Yaelam, and Korah.
\v 36 Elifaz’s sons were Teyman, Omar, Tsefo, Gaetam, Kenaz, Timna, and Amalek.
\v 37 Re’uel’s sons were Nahat, Zerah, Shamah, and Mizah.
\s1 Se’ir’s descendants
\r (Gen. 36:20-30)
\p
\v 38 Se’ir \add was another descendant of Esau\add*\add , and his\add* sons were Lotan, Shoval, Tsivoen, Anah, Dishon, Etser, and Dishan.
\v 39 Lotan’s sons were Hori and Homam, and Lotan’s sister was Timna.
\v 40 Shoval’s sons were Alvan, Manahat, Eyval, Shefo, and Onam. Tsivoen’s sons were Ayah and Anah.
\v 41 Anah’s son was Dishon. Dishon’s sons were Hamran, Eshban, Yitran, and Keran.
\v 42 Etser’s sons were Bilhan, Za’avan, and Ya’akan. Dishan’s sons were Uz and Aran.
\s1 Edom’s rulers
\r (Gen. 36:31-43)
\p
\v 43 These are the kings who ruled the Edom region \add (where Se’ir’s descendants lived)\add* before any kings ruled over Israel: Bela son of Beor, and his city’s name was Dinhavah.
\v 44 When Bela died, Zerah’s son Yovav from Batserah \add city\add* replaced him as king.
\v 45 When Yovav died, Husham replaced him as king. He was from the region where the Teyman people group lived.
\v 46 When Husham died, Bedad’s son Hadad replaced him as king. His city’s name was Avit. Hadad defeated the Midian people group in the Moab region.
\v 47 When Hadad died, Samlah from Masrekah \add city\add* replaced him as king.
\v 48 When Samlah died, Shaul from Rehovot-on-the-river replaced him as king.
\v 49 When Shaul died, Akbor’s son Baal-Hanan replaced him as king.
\v 50 When Baal-Hanan died, Hadad replaced him as king. His city’s name was Paeu. His wife’s name was Meheytavel—she was the daughter of Matred and the granddaughter of Mey-Zahav.
\p
\v 51 Then Hadad died.
\p The leaders of the Edom people group were Chiefs Timna, Alvah, Yetet,
\v 52 Oholivamah, Elah, Pinon,
\v 53 Kenaz, Teyman, Mivtsar,
\v 54 Magdiyel, and Iram. Those were the chiefs of Edom.
\c 2
\s1 Jacob’s descendants
\p
\v 1 These were Israel’s sons: Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah, Issachar, and Zebulun,
\v 2 Dan, Joseph, Benjamin, Naphtali, Gad, and Asher.
\s1 Judah’s descendants
\p
\v 3 Judah’s sons (with the Canaanite woman Bat-Shua) were Er, Onan, and Shelah. (Judah’s eldest son, Er, was evil in Yahweh’s eyes, and he killed him.)
\v 4 His daughter-in-law Tamar had \add twin boys named\add* Perez and Zerah, so altogether Judah had five sons.
\p
\v 5 Perez’s sons were Hezron and Hamul.
\v 6 Zerah had five sons: Zimri, Eytan, Heyman, Kalkol, and Dara.
\v 7 \add Zimri had a son named Karmi.\add* Karmi had a son named Akar who brought trouble into Israel by violating a ban on devoted things.
\v 8 Eytan’s son was Azaryah.
\s1 King David’s ancestors
\p
\v 9 Hezron had three sons: Yerahme’el, Ram, and Keluvay.\f + \fr 2:9 \ft In the Hebrew, ‘Keluvay’ is called ‘Caleb’ below.\f*
\p
\v 10 Ram was the father of Amminadab, and Amminadab was the father of Nahshon—a leader of the tribe of Judah.
\v 11 Nahshon was the father of Salma. Salma was the father of Boaz.
\v 12 Boaz was the father of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
\v 13 Jesse had his son Eliav first, Avinadav second, Shimea third.
\v 14 Netanel fourth, Raday fifth,
\v 15 Otsem sixth, then David last.
\v 16 Their sisters were Tseruyah and Abigail. Tseruyah’s three sons were Avishay, Joab, and Asahel.
\v 17 Abigail had Amasa. Amasa’s father was Yeter, a descendant of Ishmael.
\s1 The descendants of Caleb
\p
\v 18 Hezron’s \add third\add* son Caleb had sons with Azuvah, one of his wives. The sons were Yesher, Shovav, and Ardon. (The other wife’s name was Yerieot.)
\v 19 When Azuvah died, Caleb married Efrat, and they had a son named Hur.
\v 20 Hur was the father of Uri, and Uri was the father of Betsalel.
\p
\v 21 Later, when Hezron was sixty years old, he had sexual intercourse with a daughter of Makir from the Gilead region and married her. Hezron’s new wife gave birth to \add their son\add* Seguv.
\v 22 Seguv was the father of Yair, and Yair controlled twenty-three cities in the Gilead region.
\v 23 But Geshur and Aram captured Havot-Ya’ir and Kenat from them, plus their surrounding towns—sixty towns \add in all,\add* populated by descendants of Makir (the father of Gilead).
\v 24 After Hezron’s death in Caleb-Efratah, his wife Abijah gave birth to his son Assyria, the father of Tekoa.
\s1 Yerahme’el’s descendants
\p
\v 25 Hezron’s oldest son was Yerahme’el \add and\add* his sons were Ram (the oldest), Bunah, Oren, Otsem, and Ahiyah.
\v 26 Yerahme’el had another wife named Atarah. She was the mother of Onam.
\v 27 The sons of Yerahme’el’s oldest son, Ram, were Ma’ats, Yamin, and Eker.
\v 28 Onam’s sons were Shamay and Yada, and Shamay’s sons were Nadav and Avishur.
\p
\v 29 Avishur’s wife was Avihayil, and their sons were Ahban and Molid.
\v 30 Nadav’s sons were Seled and Apayim. (Seled died childless.)
\v 31 Apayim’s son was Yishei, Yishei’s son was Sheshan, and Sheshan’s son was Ahlay.
\p
\v 32 The sons of Shamay’s brother Yada were Yeter and Jonathan. (Yeter died childless.)
\v 33 Jonathan’s sons were Pelet and Zaza.
\m Those were the descendants of Yerahme’el.
\p
\v 34 Sheshan had no sons, only daughters. He had a \add male\add* servant from Egypt whose name was Yarha,
\v 35 and Sheshan gave his daughter to him for a wife, and they had a son Atay.
\v 36 Atay was the father of Nathan, and Nathan was the father of Zavad.
\v 37 Zavad was the father of Eflal, and Eflal was the father of Obed.
\v 38 Obed was the father of Jehu, and Jehu was the father of Azaryah.
\v 39 Azaryah was the father of Helets, and Helets was the father of Eleasah.
\v 40 Eleasah was the father of Sismay, and Sismay was the father of Shalum.
\v 41 Shalum was the father of Yekamyah, and Yekamyah was the father of Elishama.
\s1 The other descendants of Caleb
\p
\v 42 The oldest son of Yerahme’el’s brother Caleb was Meysha. Meysha was the father of Zif, and Zif was the father of Mareshah. Mareshah was the father of Hebron.
\v 43 Hebron’s sons were Korah, Tapuah, Rekem, and Shema.
\v 44 Shema was the father of Raham. Raham was the father of Yarekeam. Rekem was the father of Shamay.
\v 45 Shamay’s son was Maeon, and Maeon was the father of Beyt-Tsur.
\v 46 Caleb had a slave-wife named Eyfah who gave birth to Haran, Motsa, and Gazez. Haran had a son \add who he also named\add* Gazez.
\v 47 Yehday’s sons were: Regem, Jotham, Geyshan, Pelet, Eyfah, and Sha’af.
\p
\v 48 Caleb had \add another\add* slave-wife named Ma’akah who gave birth to Shever and Tirhanah.
\v 49 She also gave birth to Sha’af (the father of Madmanah), Sheva (father of Makbena and Givea), and Caleb’s daughter Aksah.
\p
\v 50 These people were \add also\add* descendants of Caleb: \add His other wife\add* Efratah had a son Hur. Hur’s sons were Shoval (founder of Kiryat-Yearim \add city\add*),
\v 51 Salma (founder of Bethlehem, \add and\add* Haref (founder of Beyt-Gader).
\v 52 The descendants of Shoval (founder of Kiryat-Yearim) were Haroeh, half of the Manahatim,
\v 53 and the clans from Kiryat-Yearim: the Yitrim, the Putim, the Shumatim, and the Mishraim. From those came the Tsor’atim and Eshtaolim.
\v 54 Salma’s descendants were \add the people of\add* Bethlehem, the Netofatim, the clan of Atrot-Beyt-Yo’av, and half of the Manahatim, \add who were also\add* Tsor’im.
\v 55 The clans of the scribes lived at Yaebets: the Tiratim, the Shimeatim, and the Sukatim. Those are the Keynim who descended from Hamat, the ancestor of Rekav’s family.
\c 3
\s1 King David’s children
\p
\v 1 These were David’s sons who were born to him in Hebron: the first was Amnon by Ahinoam the Yizre’eli, the second Daniel by Abigail the Karmeli,
\v 2 the third Absalom, the son of Ma’akah (daughter of King Talmay of Geshur), the fourth Adonijah, the son of Hagit,
\v 3 the fifth Shefatyah by Avital, the sixth Yitream by his wife Eglah.
\m
\v 4 Six were born to David in Hebron where he reigned for seven and a half years.
\p Then he reigned in Jerusalem for thirty-three years
\v 5 and these were born to him there: Bat-Shua (\add also known as Bathsheba\add*, daughter of Amiyel) gave birth to four sons: Shimea, Shovav, Nathan, and Solomon.
\v 6 \add Other sons were\add* Yavehar, Elishama, Elifelet,
\v 7 Nogah, Nefeg, Yafia,
\v 8 Elishama, Elyada, and Elifelet. (Nine in all.)
\v 9 David also had sons from his slave-wives. Their sister was Tamar.
\s1 Descendants of King Solomon
\p
\v 10 Solomon had a son Rehoboam, he had a son Abijah, he had a son Asa, he had a son Jehoshaphat,
\v 11 he had a son Joram, he had a son Ahazyah, he had a son Yoash,
\v 12 he had a son Amatsyah, he had a son Azaryah, he had a son Jotham,
\v 13 he had a son Ahaz, he had a son Hezekiah, he had a son Manasseh,
\v 14 he had a son Amon, \add and\add* he had a son Josiah.
\v 15 Josiah had four sons: The oldest was Yohanan, second was Yehoyakim, third was Zedekiah, \add and\add* the fourth was Shalum.
\v 16 Yehoyakim had a son Jeconiah, \add and\add* he had a son Zedekiah.
\s1 Descendants of King Jeconiah
\p
\v 17 Jeconiah \add was captured and taken to Babylon\add*. His sons were Shealtiel,
\v 18 Malkiram, Pedayah, Shenatsar, Yekamyah, Hoshama, and Nedavyah.
\v 19 Pedayah’s sons were Zerubbabel and Shimei. \add Two of\add* Zerubbabel’s sons were Meshulam and Hananyah, and their sister was Shelomot.
\v 20 Zerubbabel’s five \add other\add* sons were Hashuvah, Ohel, Berechiah, Hasadyah, and Yushav-Hesed.
\v 21 Hananyah’s descendants were Pelatyah, Isaiah, \add and\add* the sons of Refayah, of Arnan, of Obadiah, and of Shekanyah.
\v 22 Shekanyah’s six descendants were Shemayah and Shemayah’s sons Hatush, Yigal, Bariah, Nearyah, and Shafat.
\v 23 Nearyah’s three sons were Elyehoeynay, Hizkiyah, and Azrikam.
\v 24 Elyehoeynay’s seven sons were Hodavyah, Elyashiv, Pelayah, Akuv, Yohanan, Delayah, and Anani.
\c 4
\s1 The other descendants of Judah
\p
\v 1 Judah’s sons were Perez, Hezron, Karmi, Hur, and Shoval.
\v 2 Shoval had a son Reayah, Reayah had a son Yahat, and Yahat had Ahumay and Lahad. Those were the ancestors of the Tsor’atim.
\v 3 Jezreel, Yishma, and Yidbas started the clans that lived in Eytam \add city\add*, and they had a sister named Hatslelponi.
\v 4 Hur was the first son born to \add his mother\add* Efratah, \add and\add* he founded Bethlehem \add city\add*. Hur had these descendants: Penuel and Ezer. Penuel started the clans that lived in Gedor \add city\add*, and Ezer started the clans that lived in Hushah \add city\add*.
\v 5 \add Hezron’s son\add* Assyria, the father of Tekoa, had two wives, Helah and Na’arah,
\v 6 and Na’arah bore him Ahuzam, Hefer, Teymeni, and Ha’ahashtari.
\v 7 \add Assyria and his wife\add* Helah’s sons were Tseret, Yitshar, Etnan,
\v 8 and Kots. Kots was the father of Anuv and Tsovevah, and the ancestor of the clans descended from Harum’s son Aharhel.
\p
\v 9 Yaebets was more respected than his brothers. His mother had named him Yaebets \add (which sounds like the word meaning ‘pain’)\add*, saying, “It’s because I bore him in pain.”
\v 10 Yaebets called out to Israel’s god, saying, “Oh, please give me a real blessing, and help me and enlarge my territory, and don’t do anything to harm me, so that I won’t be in pain.” And God did what Yaebets requested.
\p
\v 11 \add Another of Judah’s\add* \add descendants was\add* Shuhah. His brother Keluv was the father of Mehir. Mehir was the father of Eshton.
\v 12 Eshton was the father of Beyt-Rafa, Paseah, and Tehinah. Tehinah founded Nahash city, but lived in Rekah.
\p
\v 13 Kenaz’s sons were Otniel and Serayah. And Otniel’s sons were Hatat \add and Me’onotay\add*.\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Not in the Hebrew, but ‘sons’ was plural, so it seems that it may have been accidentally dropped out of the text.\f*
\v 14 Me’onotay had a son Ofrah, and Serayah fathered Joab, the founder of Craftsmen’s valley, because they were craftsmen.
\p
\v 15 The sons of Caleb Yefuneh’s son) were Iru, Elah, and Na’am, and Elah’s son was Kenaz.
\p
\v 16 Yehalel’el’s sons were Zif, Zifah, Tirya, and Asarel.
\p
\v 17 Ezra’s sons were Yeter, Mered, Efer, and Yalon. And Mered’s wife Bityah conceived Miriam, Shamay, and Yishbah, the father of Eshtemoa.
\v 18 She was one of Pharaoh’s daughters. Mered’s wife, who was a descendant of Judah, gave birth to Jared the father of Gedor, Hever the father of Sokoh, and Yekutiel the father of Zanoah.
\v 19 The sons of Hodiyah’s wife (Naham’s sister) were the father of Ke’ilah the Garmi and the father of Eshtemoa the Ma’akati.
\p
\v 20 Shim’on’s sons were Amnon, Rinah, Ben-Hanan, and Tulon.
\p Yishei’s sons were Zohet and Ben-Zohet.
\s1 Shelah’s descendants
\p
\v 21 The sons of Shelah (one of Judah’s sons) were Er (father of Lekah), Laedah (father of Mareshah and the clans who worked with linen at Beyt-Eshbea),
\v 22 Yokim and the men of Kozava, and Yoash and Saraf, who ruled in Moab and Yashuvi-Lehem. (All that is from ancient records.)
\v 23 They were the potters who lived in Netaeim and Gederah—they lived there and worked for the king.
\s1 The descendants of Simeon
\p
\v 24 Simeon’s sons were Nemuel, Yamin, Yariv, Zerah, and Shaul.
\v 25 Shaul’s son was Shalum. Shalum’s son was Mivsam. Mivsam’s son was Mishma.
\v 26 The \add following were\add* Mishma’s descendants: Mishma’s son was Hamuel. Hamuel’s son was Zakur. Zakur’s son was Shimei.
\v 27 Shimei had sixteen sons and six daughters, but none of his brothers had many children, so Simeon’s descendants were never as numerous as those of \add his younger brother\add* Judah.
\p
\v 28 Simeon’s descendants lived in \add these cities and towns\add*: Beersheba, Moladah, Hatsar-Shual,
\v 29 Bilhah, Etsem, Tolad,
\v 30 Betuel, Hormah, Tsiklag,
\v 31 Beyt-Hamarkavot, Hatsar-Susim, Beyt-Biri, and Sha’arayim. They lived in those places until David became king.
\v 32 They \add also\add* lived in five villages: Eytam, Ayin, Rimon, Token, and Ashan,
\v 33 and all the villages around these towns as far as Baal. Those were their settlements, and they kept their genealogical records.
\p
\v 34 \add Their clan leaders were\add* Meshovav, Yamlek, Yoshah the son of Amatsyah,
\v 35 Joel, Jehu the son of Yoshivyah, the son of Serayah, the son of Asiyel,
\v 36 as well as Elyehoeynay, Ya’akovah, Yeshohayah, Asayah, Adiel, Yeshimael, Benayah,
\v 37 and Ziza the son of Shifei, the son of Alon, the son of Yedayah, the son of Shimri, the son of Shemayah.
\v 38 Those who are named above were the leaders of their clans, and their \add extended\add* families increased greatly in number,
\v 39 and they went to the entrance of Gedor (to the east of the valley) in search of pasture for their flocks.
\v 40 They found good, fertile pasture, and the land was wide, and quiet and peaceful, because Ham’s descendants had lived there previously.
\p
\v 41 Those listed by name went \add there\add* in the days of Judah’s King Hezekiah and struck down the tents of the Meunim who were found there. They completely annihilated them (to this day), and they lived \add there\add* in their place because there was pasture for their flocks there.
\v 42 Then five hundred of their men who were descendants of Simeon went to Mt. Se’ir, led by Yishei’s sons Pelatyah, Nearyah, Refayah, and Uziel.
\v 43 They \add also\add* struck down the rest of the Amalekites who escaped, and they’ve lived there to this day.
\c 5
\s1 Reuben’s descendants
\p
\v 1 Reuben was the first \add child\add* born to Israel \add also known as Jacob\add* \add )\add*, but when he had sex with one of his father’s slave-wives, his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph, another one of Israel’s sons. So Reuben wasn’t recorded as inheriting the birthright,
\v 2 and although Judah was stronger than his \add other\add* brothers and would be the son to produce a ruler, the birthright went to Joseph.
\v 3 The sons of Israel’s eldest son Reuben were Hanok, Palu, Hezron, and Karmi.
\p
\v 4 \add Another of Reuben’s descendants was\add* Joel, and his son was Shemayah. Shemayah’s son was Gog. Gog’s son was Shimei.
\v 5 Shimei’s son was Micah. Micah’s son was Reayah. Reayah’s son was Baal.
\v 6 Baal’s son was Be’erah, who became a leader of the Re’uvenim, \add but\add* the Assyrian King Tiglat-Pileser captured him and took him back to Assyria.
\v 7 His brothers by their clans, as listed in their genealogical records: Yeiel the leader, Zechariah,
\v 8 Bela son of Azaz, son of Shema, son of Joel. Reuben’s clan lived near Aroer \add city\add* \add as far north as the cities of\add* Nevo and Baal-Me’on.
\v 9 \add Some of them lived further\add* east, as far as the edge of the desert that extends to the Euphrates river, because their livestock had multiplied \add back\add* in the Gilead region.
\v 10 (In the days of \add King\add* Saul, they’d battled with the Hagrim and defeated them, so then they’d lived in their tents all over eastern Gilead.)
\s1 Gad’s descendants
\p
\v 11 Gad’s descendants lived opposite them in the Bashan region as far as Salkah:
\v 12 Joel was their leader, Shafam was second-in-command, others were Ya’anay and Shafat in Bashan.
\v 13 Their cousins who were leaders of seven clans were Michael, Meshulam, Sheba, Yoray, Ya’akan, Ziea, and Eber.
\v 14 Those were descendants of Avihayil, the son of Huri, the son of Yaroah, the son of Gilead, the son of Michael, the son of Yeshishay, the son of Yahdo, the son of Buz.
\v 15 Ahi was the son of Avdiel, the son of Guni, head of their clans.
\v 16 Those descendants of Gad lived in towns in the Gilead and Bashan \add regions\add*, and on all the pastureland throughout the Sharon plain.
\v 17 All of them were listed in the genealogies in the days of Judah’s King Jotham and Israel’s King Jeroboam.
\s1 The eastern warriors
\p
\v 18 There were 44,760 warriors descended from strong men in the Reuben and Gad tribes and the \add eastern\add* half of the Manasseh tribe. They all carried shields and swords, and bows and arrows, and were all trained to fight in battles.
\v 19 They went to war against the Hagrim, Yetur, Nafish, and Nodav,
\v 20 and were helped because they called out to God in the battle. So with God answering their prayers because of their trust in him, they were able to defeat the Hagrim and their allies.
\v 21 They captured their livestock, including 50,000 camels, 250,000 sheep, and 2,000 donkeys, as well as 100,000 people,
\v 22 because many were killed as a result of God’s assistance. Then they lived in that area until the exile.
\s1 Manasseh’s descendants
\p
\v 23 The descendants of the Manasseh half-tribe lived in the Bashan region as far as Baal-Hermon and Senir, and Mt. Hermon, \add and\add* they increased in number.
\v 24 Their clan leaders were Efer, Yishei, Eliel, Azriel, Jeremiah, Hodavyah, and Yahdiyel. They were all powerful and famous warriors, and leaders of their clans.
\s1 The exile of the eastern tribes
\p
\v 25 But they disobeyed their ancestors’ god, and they prostituted themselves to the gods of the peoples that God had destroyed ahead of them in that region.
\v 26 So Israel’s god stirred up the spirit of the Assyrian King Pul (also known as Tiglat-Pileser). He took them into exile—that is, the Re’uvenim, the Gadim, and the Manasseh half-tribe. Then he took them to Halah, Havor, Hara, and the Gozan river (\add where they still are\add* to this day).
\c 6
\s1 Levi’s descendants
\p
\v 1 Levi’s sons were Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.
\p
\v 2 Kohat’s sons were Amram, Yitshar, Hebron, and Uziel.
\p
\v 3 Amram’s children were Miriam, Aaron, and Moses.
\p Aaron’s sons were Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar.
\p
\v 4 Eleazar was the father of Pinhas, \add and\add* Pinhas was the father of Avishua.
\v 5 Avishua was the father of Buki, and Buki was the father of Uzi.
\v 6 Uzi was the father of Zerahyah, and Zerahyah was the father of Merayot.
\v 7 Merayot fathered Amaryah, and Amaryah fathered Ahituv.
\v 8 Ahituv was the father of Zadok, and Zadok was the father of Ahima’ats.
\v 9 Ahima’ats was the father of Azaryah, and Azaryah was the father of Yohanan.
\v 10 Yohanan was the father of Azaryah. Azaryah was a priest in the temple that Solomon built in Jerusalem.
\v 11 Azaryah was the father of Amaryah, and Amaryah was the father of Ahituv.
\v 12 Ahituv was the father of Zadok, and Zadok was the father of Shalum.
\v 13 Shalum was the father of Hilkiyah, and Hilkiyah was the father of Azaryah.
\v 14 Azaryah was the father of Serayah, and Serayah was the father of Yehotsadak.
\p
\v 15 Yehotsadak was included when Yahweh sent Nebuchadnezzar to take Judah which includes Jerusalem) into exile.
\s1 Levi’s other descendants
\p
\v 16 Levi’s sons were Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.
\v 17 The names of Gereshon’s sons were Livni and Shimei.
\v 18 Kohat’s sons were Amram, Yitshar, Hebron, and Uziel.
\v 19 Merari’s sons were Mahli and Mushi.
\p \add Here’s a list of\add* Levi’s descendants who became leaders of their clans:
\p
\v 20 Gereshon’s \add oldest\add* son was Livni, Livni’s son was Yahat, \add and\add* Yahat’s son was Zimah.
\v 21 Zimah’s son was Yoah, Yoah’s son was Ido, Ido’s son was Zerah, \add and\add* Zerah’s son was Ye’eteray.
\p
\v 22 \add Another line of Kohat’s descendants started with his son Amminadab.\add* Amminadab’s son was Korah. Korah’s son was Asir.
\v 23 Asir’s son was Elkanah. Elkanah’s son was Evyasaf. Evyasaf’s son was Asir.
\v 24 Asir’s \add son was Tahat.\add* Tahat’s son was Uriel. Uriel’s son was Uzziah. Uzziah’s son was Shaul.
\p
\v 25 Elkanah’s sons were Amasay, Ahimot,
\v 26 and a son also named Elkanah. Elkanah’s son was Tsuf. Tsuf’s son was Nahat.
\v 27 Nahat’s \add son was Eliav.\add* Eliav’s son was Yeroham. Yeroham’s son was Elkanah.
\p
\v 28 Samuel had sons: his oldest son \add Joel\add* and his other son Abijah.
\p
\v 29 Merari’s descendants started with Mahli. Mahli’s son was Livni. Livni’s son was Shimei. Shimei’s son was Uza.
\v 30 Uza’s \add son was Shimea.\add* Shimea’s son was Hagiyah. Hagiyah’s son was Asayah.
\s1 The temple musicians
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 31 These \add are the men that\add* David put in charge of music in Yahweh’s residence, after the \add sacred\add* box was placed there.
\v 32 They were the ones who’d ministered in the sacred tent (\add also called\add* ‘the meeting tent’) with music until Solomon built the temple in Jerusalem. They performed their duties according to their instructions.
\p
\v 33 Here’s a list of the musicians and their sons:
\li1 From Kohat’s descendants there was Heyman, the leader of the singers.
\li1 Heyman was Joel’s son.
\li1 Joel was Samuel’s son.
\li1
\v 34 Samuel was Elkanah’s son.
\li1 Elkanah was Yeroham’s son.
\li1 Yeroham was Eliel’s son.
\li1 Eliel was Toah’s son.
\li1
\v 35 Toah was Tsuf’s son.
\li1 Tsuf was Elkanah’s son.
\li1 Elkanah was Mahat’s son.
\li1 Mahat was Amasay’s son.
\li1
\v 36 Amasay was Elkanah’s son.
\li1 Elkanah was Joel’s son.
\li1 Joel was Azaryah’s son.
\li1 Azaryah was Zephaniah’s son.
\li1
\v 37 Zephaniah was Tahat’s son.
\li1 Tahat was Asir’s son.
\li1 Asir was Evyasaf’s son.
\li1 Evyasaf was Korah’s son.
\li1
\v 38 Korah was Yitshar’s son.
\li1 Yitshar was Kohat’s son.
\li1 Kohat was Levi’s son.
\li1 Levi was the son of \add Jacob\add* \add or Jacob, who was later named\add* Israel/\add Israel)\add*.
\p
\v 39 Heyman’s cousin Asaf’s \add group\add* stood to the right. Asaf was Berechiah’s son. Berechiah was Shimea’s son.
\v 40 Shimea was Michael’s son. Michael was Ba’aseyah’s son. Ba’aseyah was Malkiyah’s son.
\v 41 Malkiyah was Etni’s son. Etni was Zerah’s son. Zerah was Adayah’s son.
\v 42 Adayah was Eytan’s son. Eytan was Zimah’s son. Zimah was Shimei’s son.
\v 43 Shimei was Yahat’s son. Yahat was Gereshon’s son. Gereshon was Levi’s son.
\p
\v 44 Heyman’s cousin Merari’s \add group\add* stood to the left. Eytan was Kishi’s son. Kishi was Avdi’s son. Avdi was Maluk’s son.
\v 45 Maluk was Hashavyah’s son. Hashavyah was Uzziah’s son. Uzziah was Hilkiyah’s son.
\v 46 Hilkiyah was Amtsi’s son. Amtsi was Bani’s son. Bani was Shemer’s son.
\v 47 Shemer was Mahli’s son. Mahli was Mushi’s son. Mushi was Merari’s son. Merari was Levi’s son.
\p
\v 48 Their other Levite cousins were assigned to various ministries serving in God’s residence in the sacred tent.
\v 49 It was Aaron and his sons who were responsible for offering the sacrifices on the altar for burnt offerings and on the altar for incense, for all the work of the holiest place, and for making atonement for Israel, according to everything that God’s servant Moses had instructed.
\v 50 These were Aaron’s descendants: Aaron’s son was Eleazar. Eleazar’s son was Pinhas. Pinhas’s son was Avishua.
\v 51 Avishua’s son was Buki. Buki’s son was Uzi. Uzi’s son was Zerahyah.
\v 52 Zerahyah’s son was Merayot. Merayot’s son was Amaryah. Amaryah’s son was Ahituv.
\v 53 Ahituv’s son was Zadok, and Zadok’s son was Ahima’ats.
\s1 The Levites’ settlements
\p
\v 54 Here’s a list of places where Aaron’s descendants lived. Kohat’s descendants were the first to be allotted property.
\v 55 They were given Hebron \add city\add* in Judah and the surrounding pastureland,
\v 56 except that Caleb received the fields \add farther from\add* the city and the villages \add around\add* the city. (Caleb was Yefuneh’s son.)
\v 57 Those descendants of Aaron were given Hebron (a refuge city), as well as these towns and their \add nearby\add* pastureland: Livnah, Yatir, Eshtemoa,
\v 58 Hilen, Devir,
\v 59 Ashan, and Beyt-Shemesh.
\v 60 From the Benjamin tribe, they received the cities of Geva, Alemet, and Anatot and their pasturelands. Altogether \add those Kohatite\add* clans received thirteen cities,
\v 61 and the remaining Kohatite clans received ten cities from the clans of the Manasseh tribe.
\p
\v 62 The clans of Gereshon’s descendants received thirteen cities from the Issachar, Asher, and Naphtali tribes, and the part of the Manasseh tribe that lived in the Bashan \add region\add* \add across the Jordan\add* \add river\add*.
\v 63 The clans of Merari’s descendants received twelve cities from the Reuben, Gad, and Zebulun tribes.
\v 64 The Israelite leaders allotted those towns and the nearby pasturelands to the Levites.
\v 65 They also allotted them the cities from the Judah, Simeon, and Benjamin tribes that were listed above.
\v 66 Some of Kohat’s descendants received cities from the Ephraim tribe.
\v 67 They \add also\add* gave them these cities with the surrounding pasturelands: Shechem (a refuge city), Gezer,
\v 68 Yakemeam, Beyt-Horon,
\v 69 Ayalon, and Gath-Rimon.
\v 70 Kohat’s other descendants received \add the cities of\add* Aner and Balaam and the nearby pastureland from the Manasseh half-tribe \add (that lives west of the Jordan river)\add*.
\p
\v 71 Gereshon’s descendants also received, from the part of the Manasseh tribe \add that lived across the Jordan river\add*, \add two cities\add* and pastureland near them: Golan in the Bashan \add region\add*, and Ashtarot.
\p
\v 72 From the Issachar \add tribe they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Kedesh, Davrat,
\v 73 Ramot, and Anem.
\v 74 From the Asher tribe \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Mashal, Avdon,
\v 75 Hukok, and Rehov.
\p
\v 76 From the Naphtali tribe \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Kedesh (in the Galilee \add region\add*), Hamon, and Kiryatayim.
\p
\v 77 Merari’s descendants \add received these cities and\add* their nearby pasturelands from the Zebulun tribe: Rimon and Tavor.
\p
\v 78 From the Reuben tribe across the Jordan \add river\add* (opposite Jericho, \add they were allotted these cities and towns\add* and their nearby pastureland: Betser in the wilderness, Yahats,
\v 79 Kedemot, and Mofa’at.
\p
\v 80 From the Gad tribe, \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Ramot in the Gilead \add region\add*, Mahanayim,
\v 81 Heshbon, and Yazer.
\c 7
\s1 The descendants of Issachar
\p
\v 1 Issachar’s four sons were Tola, Puah, Yashuv, and Shimron.
\v 2 Tola’s sons were Uzi, Refayah, Yeriel, Yahmay, Yivsam, and Samuel, \add who all became\add* leaders of their clans. There were strong warriors in the record of Tola’s descendants in each generation. (During the time when David was Israel’s king, there were 22,600 such men.)
\p
\v 3 Uzi’s son was Yizrahyah. Yizrahyah’s five sons were Michael, Obadiah, Joel, and Yishiyah. They were all leaders of their clans.
\v 4 Along with them, according to their family records, were 36,000 troops in the army, because they had numerous wives and sons.
\v 5 Altogether, Issachar’s clans had 87,000 strong warriors listed in their genealogy.
\s1 Benjamin’s descendants
\p
\v 6 Benjamin’s three sons were Bela, Beker, and Yedieael.
\v 7 Bela’s five sons were Etsbon, Uzi, Uziel, Yerimot, and Iri, who were leaders of their clans. According to their family records, they had 22,034 strong warriors.
\p
\v 8 Beker’s sons were Zemirah, Yoash, Eliezer, Elyehoeynay, Omri, Yerimot, Abijah, Anatot, and Alemet.
\v 9 Altogether, their clans had 20,200 strong warriors listed in their genealogy.
\p
\v 10 Yedieael’s son was Bilhan. Bilhan’s sons were Ye’ush, Benjamin, Ehud, Kena’anah, Zeytan, Tarshish, and Ahishahar.
\v 11 All those descendants of Yedieael were clan leaders and strong warriors, having 17,200 men ready to go to war.
\p
\v 12 The Shupim and Hupim were descendants of Ir, \add and\add* the Hushim were descendants of Aher.
\s1 The descendants of Naphtali
\p
\v 13 Naphtali’s sons were Yahtsiel, Guni, Yetser, and Shalum. (\add They were\add* descendants of \add Jacob’s\add* \add slave-wife\add* Bilhah.)
\s1 Manasseh’s descendants
\p
\v 14 Manasseh had sons by his Syrian Heb. Aramean) slave-wife. She gave birth to Asriel and Makir (who fathered Gilead).
\v 15 Makir married a wife from the Hupim and Shupim. (His sister’s name was Ma’akah.) Another descendant was Tselofehad, and he had daughters.
\v 16 Makir’s wife (\add also called\add* Ma’akah) bore a son and named him Peresh (and named his brother Sheresh) and his sons were Ulam and Rekem.
\v 17 Ulam’s son was Bedan. Those were the descendants of Gilead (Makir’s son and Manasseh’s grandson).
\p
\v 18 Gilead’s sister was Hamoleket. She was the mother of Ishehod, Aviezer, and Mahlah.
\p
\v 19 \add Another of Gilead’s sons was\add* Shemida, whose sons were Ahyan, Shechem, Likhi, and Anieam.
\s1 Ephraim’s descendants
\p
\v 20 These are Ephraim’s descendants:
\p One of Ephraim’s sons was Shutelah. Shutelah’s son was Bered. Bered’s son was Tahat. Tahat’s son was Eleadah. Eleadah’s son \add was also named\add* Tahat.
\v 21 Tahat’s son was Zavad. Zavad’s son was Shutelah. Ephraim’s other sons, Ezer and Elead, went to Gath \add city\add* to steal some cattle but were killed by some of the natives of that area.
\v 22 Their father Ephraim mourned for quite some time, and his relatives came to comfort him.
\v 23 Then he slept with his wife and she got pregnant and gave birth to a son. Ephraim named him Beriah \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘trouble’)\add*, because of the recent tragedy in their family.
\p
\v 24 Ephraim’s daughter was She’erah and she founded \add three towns\add*: Lower Beyt-Horon, Upper Beyt-Horon, and Uzen-She’erah.
\p
\v 25 \add Another of Ephraim’s sons\add* was Refah. Refah’s son was Reshef. Reshef’s son was Telah. Telah’s son was Tahan.
\v 26 Tahan’s son was Laedan. Laedan’s son was Amihud. Amihud’s son was Elishama.
\v 27 Elishama’s son was Nun. Nun’s son was Joshua \add or Joshua, who led Israel’s\add* \add descendants after Moses\add* \add died)\add*.
\p
\v 28 \add This is a list of\add* the cities and areas where Ephraim’s descendants lived: Bethel and the nearby villages, Na’aran to the east, Gezer to the west and the nearby villages, and Shechem and the nearby villages extending \add north\add* as far as Ayah and its villages.
\p
\v 29 Along the border of the area where Manasseh’s descendants lived were these towns: Beyt-Shean, Ta’anak, Megiddo, Dor, and all the nearby villages. The people who lived in all those places were descendants of Jacob’s son Joseph.
\s1 Asher’s descendants
\p
\v 30 Asher’s sons were Yimnah, Yishvah, Yishvi, and Beriah. Their sister was Serah.
\p
\v 31 Beriah’s sons were Hever and Malkiel. Malkiel was the father of Birzot.
\p
\v 32 Hever was the father of Yaflet, Shomer, Hotam, and their sister Shua.
\v 33 Yaflet’s sons were Pasak, Bimhal, and Ashvat.
\p
\v 34 Shomer’s sons were Ahi, Rohagah, Yehubah, and Aram.
\p
\v 35 \add Shomer’s younger\add* brother was Helem. Helem’s sons were Tsofah, Yimna, Shelesh, and Amal.
\p
\v 36 Tsofah’s sons were Suah, Harnefer, Shual, Beri, Yimrah,
\v 37 Betser, Hod, Shama, Shilshah, Yitran (\add whose other name was Yeter\add*), and Be’era.
\p
\v 38 Yeter’s sons were Yefuneh, Pispah, and Ara.
\v 39 \add Another of Asher’s descendants was\add* Ula, whose sons were Arah, Haniyel, and Ritsya.
\p
\v 40 All those were Asher’s descendants, clan leaders, chosen, very strong, leaders of leaders. And there were 26,000 men listed in their genealogy in the army.
\c 8
\s1 Benjamin’s descendants
\p
\v 1 Benjamin had sons: Bela, Ashbel, Ahrah,
\v 2 Nohah, and the fifth, Rafa.
\p
\v 3 Bela’s sons were Adar, Gera, Avihud,
\v 4 Avishua, Naaman, Ahoah,
\v 5 Gera, Shefufan, and Huram.
\p
\v 6 \add One of Gera’s sons was\add* Ehud. His descendants were leaders of their clans who lived in Geva \add city\add*, but were taken into exile to Manahat.
\v 7 Ehud’s sons were Naaman, Ahiyah, and Gera. Gera was the one who led them \add when they moved to Manahat\add*. He was the father of Uza and Ahihud.
\p
\v 8 \add Another of Benjamin’s\add* \add descendants was\add* Shaharayim, who lived in the Moab countryside. He divorced his wives Hushim and Ba’ara,
\v 9 and fathered \add sons\add* with his wife Hodesh: Yovav, Tsivya, Meysha, Malkam,
\v 10 Ye’uts, Shaveyah, and Mirmah. They all became leaders of their clans.
\v 11 (He’d fathered Avituv and Elpa’al by Hushim.)
\p
\v 12 Elpa’al’s sons were Eber, Misham, Shemer (who built Ono \add city\add*, plus Lod and its towns),
\s2 Benjamin’s descendants in Gath and Ayalon
\p
\v 13 plus Beriah and Shema, who were leaders of their clans, who lived in Ayalon \add city\add*. They forced the people who lived in Gath \add city\add* to flee.
\v 14-16 Beriah’s sons were Ahyo, Shashak, Yerimot, Zevadyah, Arad, Eder, Michael, Yishpah, and Yoha.
\s2 Benjamin’s descendants in Jerusalem
\p
\v 17-18 Elpa’al’s \add other\add* descendants included Zevadyah, Meshulam, Hizki, Hever, Yishmeray, Yizliah, and Yovav.
\p
\v 19-21 \add Another of Benjamin’s\add* \add descendants was Shimei.\add* Shimei’s descendants included Yakim, Zikri, Zavdi, Eli’eynay, Tsiletay, Eliel, Adayah, Berayah, and Shimrat.
\p
\v 22-25 Shashak’s sons were Yishpan, Eber, Eliel, Avdon, Zikri, Hanan, Hananyah, Eylam, Antotiyah, Yifdeyah, and Penuel.
\p
\v 26-27 \add Another of Benjamin’s descendants was\add* Yeroham, whose sons were Shamsheray, Sheharyah, Athaliah, Ya’areshyah, Elijah, and Zikri.
\p
\v 28 All those descendants of Elpa’al (Shaharayim’s son) were leaders of their clans according to their genealogies. They \add all\add* lived in Jerusalem.
\s2 Benjamin’s descendants in Gibeon
\p
\v 29 \add Another of Benjamin’s\add* \add descendants was Yeiel\add*—he was a founder of Gibeon \add city\add*. His wife was Ma’akah.
\v 30 His sons, \add starting with\add* the eldest, were Avdon, Tsur, Kish, Baal, Nadav,
\v 31 Gedor, Ahyo, and Zeker.
\v 32 Miklot \add (another of Yeiel’s sons)\add* was Shimeah’s father. All those descendants of Yeiel also lived in Jerusalem near their relatives.
\s1 The descendants of King Saul
\p
\v 33 Ner was the father of Kish. Kish was the father of \add King\add* Saul. Saul was the father of Jonathan, Malkishua, Avinadav, and Eshba’al.
\v 34 Jonathan’s son was Meriv-Baal. Meriv-Baal was the father of Micah.
\p
\v 35 Micah’s sons were Piton, Melek, Ta’area, and Ahaz.
\v 36 Ahaz was the father of Yeho’adah. Yeho’adah was the father of Alemet, Azmavet, and Zimri. Zimri was the father of Motsa.
\v 37 Motsa was the father of Binea. Rafa was Binea’s son. Eleasah was Rafa’s son. Atsel was Eleasah’s son.
\p
\v 38 Atsel had a total of six sons, named Azrikam, Bokru, Ishmael, Shearyah, Obadiah, and Hanan.
\v 39 Atsel had a brother, Eshek. Eshek’s oldest son was Ulam. His other sons were Ye’ush and Elifelet.
\p
\v 40 Ulam’s sons were powerful warriors who were also archers. Altogether they had 150 sons and grandsons.
\p All those were Benjamin’s descendants.
\c 9
\s1 Those who returned from captivity
\p
\v 1 So the genealogy of all Israelites was listed—yes, written on the scroll of Israel’s kings.
\p Then Judah was taken into exile to Babylon because of their unfaithfulness.
\v 2 The first to return and resettle their land and their cities were \add some\add* priests, Levites, temple servants, and \add other\add* Israelites.
\v 3 \add Some from the\add* Judah, Benjamin, Ephraim, and Manasseh \add tribes also returned and\add* lived in Jerusalem, \add including the following:\add*
\p
\v 4 Utay son of Amihud (Amihud was Omri’s son, Omri was Imri’s son, Imri was Bani’s son, Bani was a descendant of Perez, Perez was Judah’s son),
\v 5 Asayah and his sons (descendants of Shilon—Asayah was the oldest),
\v 6 and Ye’uel and 690 other relatives from Zerah’s clan.
\p
\v 7 From Benjamin’s descendants, Salu (who was Meshulam’s son, Meshulam was Hodavyah’s son, Hodavyah was Senuah’s son),
\v 8 Yivneyah (Yeroham’s son), Elah (who was Uzi’s son, Uzi was Mikri’s son), and Meshulam (who was Shefatyah’s son, Shefatyah was Re’uel’s son, Re’uel was Yivniyah’s son).
\p
\v 9 In all, there were 956 of Benjamin’s descendants living in Jerusalem. All those \add above\add* were leaders of their clans.
\p
\v 10 Some of the priests \add who returned to Judah\add* were: Yedayah, Yehoyariv, Yakin,
\v 11 Azaryah, the leading official at the temple (Azaryah was Hilkiyah’s son, Hilkiyah was Meshulam’s son, Meshulam was Zadok’s son, Zadok was Merayot’s son, Merayot was Ahituv’s son),
\v 12 Adayah (who was Yeroham’s son, Yeroham was Pashhur’s son, Pashhur was Malkiyah’s son), and Ma’asay (who was Adiel’s son, Adiel was Yahzerah’s son, Yahzerah was Meshulam’s son, Meshulam was Meshilemit’s son, Meshilemit was Imer’s son).
\p
\v 13 Altogether there were 1,760 \add priests who returned to Judah\add*. They were leaders of their clans, and they were all capable and responsible to work in God’s residence.
\s1 Levites who returned
\p
\v 14 From Levi’s descendants \add who returned to Judah\add* \add there were\add*: Shemayah (who was Hashuv’s son, Hashuv was Azrikam’s son, Azrikam was Hashavyah’s son, Hashavyah was a descendant of \add Levi’s youngest son\add* Merari),
\v 15 Bakbakar, Heresh, Galal, Matanyah (who was Mika’s son, Mika was Zikri’s son, Zikri was Asaf’s son),
\v 16 Obadiah who was Shemayah’s son, Shemayah was Galal’s son, Galal was Yedutun’s son), and Berechiah who lived in one of the villages where the Netofat people lived (Berechiah was Asa’s son, Asa was Elkanah’s son).
\p
\v 17 \add From Levi’s descendants who returned to Judah and\add* who guarded the \add temple\add* gates there were Shalum, Akuv, Talmon, Ahiman, and some of their relatives. Shalum was their leader.
\v 18 Those descendents of Levi were gatekeepers at the King’s gate on the east, and their descendants still are, even to this day.
\p
\v 19 Shalum was Kore’s son, Kore was Evyasaf’s son, and Evyasaf was Korah’s son. Shalum and his relatives from his clan (Korah’s descendants) had the duty of being gatekeepers, responsible for guarding the entrances to Yahweh’s temple, as their ancestors had done.
\v 20 Previously Pinhas (Eleazar’s son) had supervised the gatekeepers. Yahweh was with Pinhas.
\p
\v 21 Zechariah Meshelemyah’s son) was gatekeeper of the entrance to the assembly tent.
\v 22 Altogether, there were 212 men chosen to guard the gates. The records of the clans in their villages listed their names. \add King\add* David and the prophet Samuel appointed them because they were dependable.
\v 23 They and their sons \add watched\add* over the gates of Yahweh’s house, that is, the sacred tent, as guards.
\v 24 There were gatekeepers on each of the \add temple’s\add* four sides: east, west, north, and south.
\v 25 Their relatives in their villages would come in from time to time \add to relieve them\add* for seven days,
\v 26 because the four leaders of the gatekeepers were Levites, and they were \add also\add* trusted to supervise the rooms and the treasuries in God’s residence.
\v 27 They would spend the night around the temple while they were in charge, and would open it with the key each morning.
\p
\v 28 Some of them monitored the utensils used in the activities—counting them as they brought them out and when they put them away \add again\add*.
\v 29 Others took care of the containers and utensils used in the sanctuary, along with the fine flour, the wine and oil, and the frankincense and spices.
\v 30 Some of the priests mixed \add the fragrances and\add* spices.
\p
\v 31 There was a Levite \add named\add* Matityah (Shalum’s oldest son, who was a descendant of Korah) whose official duty was supervising the baking of the flat breads.
\v 32 Some of Kohat’s descendants prepared the rows of bread to \add be laid out on the sacred table\add* every rest day.
\p
\v 33 Some of Levi’s descendants were \add temple musicians and\add* singers. The leaders of those families stayed in the \add temple\add* accommodations. They didn’t have other duties, because they \add were expected to\add* serve day and night.
\v 34 Those were the heads of the Levite families according to their generations, \add and\add* they lived in Jerusalem.
\s1 King Saul’s ancestors and descendants
\r (1 Chr. 8:29-38)
\p
\v 35 \add Another of Benjamin’s\add* \add descendants was\add* Yeiel. He was a founder of Gibeon \add city\add* and lived there. His wife was Ma’akah.
\v 36 His oldest son was Avdon, and his other sons were Tsur, Kish, Baal, Ner, Nadav,
\v 37 Gedor, Ahyo, Zechariah, and Miklot.
\v 38 Miklot was Shim’am’s father. They also lived near their relatives in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 39 Ner was the father of Kish. Kish was the father of \add King\add* Saul. Saul was the father of Jonathan, Malkishua, Avinadav, and Eshba’al.
\v 40 Jonathan’s son was Meriv-Baal. Meriv-Baal was the father of Micah.
\v 41 Micah’s sons were Piton, Melek, and Tahrea.
\v 42 Ahaz was the father of Yaerah. Yaerah was the father of Alemet, Azmavet, and Zimri. Zimri was the father of Motsa.
\v 43 Motsa was the father of Binea. Refayah was Binea’s son. Eleasah was Refayah’s son. Atsel was Eleasah’s son.
\p
\v 44 Atsel had six sons, named Azrikam, Bokru, Ishmael, Shearyah, Obadiah, and Hanan.
\c 10
\s1 The death of King Saul and his sons
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Sam. 31:1-13)
\p
\v 1 Now the Philistines fought against Israel, and the Israelite men fled away from the Philistines, and many were killed on Mt. Gilboa.
\v 2 Then the Philistines pursued Saul and his sons, and they killed his sons Jonathan, Avinadav, and Malkishua.
\v 3 The battle against Saul was very fierce, and then the archers spotted him and wounded him.
\v 4 Then Saul said to his equipment carrier, “Draw your sword and pierce me with it, so those uncircumcised \add Philistines\add* can’t come here and torture me.” But his equipment carrier was very scared and wasn’t willing to do it, so Saul took his sword and fell onto it.
\v 5 Then his equipment carrier, seeing that \add King\add* Saul had died, also fell on his sword and died.
\v 6 So Saul and his three sons died, thus ending his family’s rule.
\v 7 When all the Israelites living in the valley saw that the army had fled, and that Saul and his sons were dead, they abandoned their cities and fled. Then the Philistines came, and they lived there \add instead\add*.
\p
\v 8 The next day, when the Philistines came to strip the dead, they found Saul and his sons’ bodies on Mt. Gilboa.
\v 9 They stripped him and carried away his head and his armour. Then they sent \add messengers\add* all around the Philistine regions to take the news to their idols and the people.
\v 10 They put his armour in the temple of their gods, and they hung his head in \add their god\add* Dagon’s temple.
\v 11 When everyone who lived \add back\add* in Yavesh in the Gilead \add region\add* heard about everything the Philistines had done to Saul,
\v 12 all the strong men took action and retrieved the corpses of Saul and his sons, and brought them \add back\add* to Yavesh. Then they buried them under the large tree in Yavesh, and they fasted for seven days.
\v 13 So Saul died because of his disobedience—when he was unfaithful to Yahweh by not following Yahweh’s instructions, and also because he consulted a medium \add for guidance\add*
\v 14 instead of requesting Yahweh’s advice. So Yahweh killed him and handed the kingdom over to Jesse’s son David.
\c 11
\s1 David becomes king
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 5:1-10)
\p
\v 1 Then all of Israel assembled around David at Hebron, saying, “Look, we’re your flesh and blood \add relatives\add*.
\v 2 In the recent past, even when Saul was king, \em you\em* were the one who took Israel out and the one who brought us back in. Plus your god Yahweh told you, ‘You yourself will shepherd my people, Israel, and you yourself will become the leader of my Israelite people.’ ”
\v 3 So all the Israelite elders went to the king at Hebron, and David made a solemn agreement with them at Hebron in front of Yahweh. Then they anointed David as king over Israel in accordance with Yahweh’s message that was given through \add the prophet\add* Samuel.
\p
\v 4 Then David and all of Israel went to Jerusalem \add Jerusalem, which was also known as\add* Yevus because the Jebusites were living there).
\v 5 The inhabitants inside Yevus said to David, “You won’t be entering this place.” But David captured \add what became known as\add* the Zion fortress, that is, David’s city.
\v 6 He’d said, “The one who strikes down the Jebusites first will become my army commander.” Tseruyah’s son Joab went up \add over the wall\add* first, and he became head \add of the army\add*.
\v 7 David \add started\add* living in that fortress, so then they called it David’s city.
\v 8 He built the city up all around, from the terrace to the surrounding walls, and Joab restored the rest of the city.
\v 9 So David became more powerful because Commander-in-chief Yahweh helped him.
\s1 David’s powerful warriors
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 The following were the leaders of David’s powerful warriors who, together with all of Israel, strengthened his kingdom and established him as king, just as Yahweh had said.
\v 11 So these are David’s powerful warriors: Yashaveam (a Hakmoni \add and\add* the leader of the thirty), who once used his spear against three hundred men in a single battle and killed them all.
\v 12 Next was Eleazar (an Ahohi, son of Dodo) who was one of the \add top\add* three powerful warriors.
\v 13 He was with David at Pas-Damim when the Philistines had assembled there for battle near a part of the countryside where barley was growing. At first, the Israelites fled from the Philistines,
\v 14 but then they took their stand in the middle of the barley and proceeded to defeat the Philistines as Yahweh saved them through a stunning victory.
\p
\v 15 Then three of that top thirty went down to David, near the rock at Adulam’s cave. Meanwhile, Philistines had encamped in the Refaim valley.
\v 16 David was in that fortress then, and the Philistine garrison was in Bethlehem,
\v 17 and David was thirsty and asked, “Who’ll get me water to drink from the well by the Bethlehem gate?”
\v 18 So those three broke through the Philistine camp, and they drew water from the well at the Bethlehem gate, and they brought it back to David. But David wasn’t willing to drink it, and he poured it out \add on the ground as an offering\add* to Yahweh.
\v 19 “Far be it from me to drink this water in front of my god,” he said. “Could I drink the blood of these men who risked their lives to bring it?” So he wasn’t prepared to drink it. Those were \add some of the things\add* those three powerful warriors did.
\p
\v 20 Joab’s brother Avishay was the head of a group of three. He’d killed three hundred people with his spear and had a reputation among the three.\f + \fr 11:20 \ft This seems unexpectedly similar to verse 11 and might possibly indicate some confusion in the early records.\f*
\v 21 He was honoured more than the other two and became the leader of that \add second\add* group of three, but didn’t enter the \add first\add* Three.
\p
\v 22 Yehoyada’s son Benayah from Kavtse’el was a powerful warrior who did amazing things. He struck down Ariel’s two \add sons\add* from Moab. Another time, he climbed down into a pit on a snowy day and killed the lion that was trapped in the pit.
\v 23 He \add also\add* killed a huge Egyptian man—well over two metres tall. The Egyptian was carrying a spear like a weaver’s beam, but Benayah went down to \add take him on\add* with a staff. He forced the spear from the Egyptian’s grasp, and then killed him with it.
\v 24 \add So\add* that was what Benayah (Yehoyada’s son) did that gave him a reputation among the three powerful warriors.
\v 25 \add Actually,\add* he was honoured more than the Thirty, yet he didn’t make it to the \add first\add* Three, but David appointed him \add to have authority\add* over his bodyguards.
\p
\v 26 These are the names of the powerful warriors: Asahel (Joab’s brother), Elhanan (Dodo’s son from Bethlehem,
\v 27 Shamot (from Haror), Helets (from Pelon),
\v 28 Ira (Ikesh’s son from Tekoa), Aviezer (from Anatot),
\v 29 Sibkay (from Hushah), Ilay (from Ahoh),
\v 30 Maharay (from Netofah), Heled (Ba’anah’s son from Netofah),
\v 31 Itay (Rivay’s son from Giveah, which belonged to Benjamin’s descendants), Benayah (from Pireaton),
\v 32 Huray (from the valleys near \add Mt.\add* Ga’ash), Aviel (from Aravah),
\v 33 Azmavet (from Baharum), Elyahba (from Shaalvon),
\v 34 Hashem’s sons (from Gizon), Jonathan Shageh’s son from Harar),
\v 35 Ahiam (Sakar’s son from Harar), Elifal (Ur’s son),
\v 36 Hefer (from Mekerat), Ahiyah (from Pelon),
\v 37 Hetsro (from Carmel), Na’aray (Ezbay’s son),
\v 38 Joel Nathan’s brother), Mivhar (Hagri’s son),
\v 39 Tselek (the Ammonite), Naharay (from Be’erot, armour bearer for Tseruyah’s son Joab,
\v 40 Ira (the Yitri), Garev (the Yitri),
\v 41 Uriah (\add Bathsheba’s\add* \add husband\add* the Hittite), Zavad (Ahlay’s son),
\v 42 Adina (Shiza’s son from the Reuben tribe, a leader from that tribe who had thirty soldiers with him),
\v 43 Hanan (Ma’akah’s son), Yoshafat (from Mithna),
\v 44 Uziya (from Ashterat), Shama and Yeiel (Hotam’s sons from Aroer),
\v 45 Yedieael (Shimri’s son) and his brother Yoha (from Tiz),
\v 46 Eliel (from Mahavah), Yerivay and Yoshavyah (Elna’am’s sons), Yitmah (from Moab,
\v 47 Eliel and Obed, and Ya’asiyel (from Tsovah).
\c 12
\s1 The heroes accompanied to David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 These were the men who joined David in Tsiklag, when he was banished from Kish’s son Saul’s presence. (They were among the warriors who assisted David in battle
\v 2 armed with bows. \add Some\add* used slings right-handed and \add some\add* left-handed, as well as shooting arrows with a bow. \add They were\add* from \add the\add* Benjamin \add tribe\add*, Saul’s relatives.)
\v 3 Their leader was Ahiezer, then next was Yoash, both Shema’ah’s sons from Giveah \add city\add*. \add Others were:\add* Yeziel and Pelet (Azmavet’s sons), Berakah, Jehu from Anatot \add city\add*),
\v 4 Yishmaeyah (from Gibeon \add city\add*, who was the leader of the thirty greatest warriors), Jeremiah, Yahaziel, Yohanan, Yozavad (from Gederah \add city\add*),
\v 5 Eleuzay, Yerimot, Be’alyah, Shemaryah, Shefatyah (from Haruf \add city\add*),
\v 6 Elkanah, Yishiyah, Azarel, Yoezer, and Yashaveam (all Korah’s descendants),
\v 7 and Yoelah and Zevadyah (Yeroham’s sons, from Gedor \add city\add*).
\p
\v 8 Some Gadim joined David at the wilderness fortress. \add They were\add* very strong army warriors, ready for battle with shields and spears. Their faces were fierce like lions, but they could run across the hills like gazelles.
\v 9 Their leader was Ezer, then Obadiah, then Eliav.
\v 10 Next was Mishmanah, then Jeremiah,
\v 11 Atay, Eliel,
\v 12 Yohanan, Elzavad,
\v 13 \add another\add* Jeremiah, then the eleventh was Makbanay.
\p
\v 14 Those men from the Gad tribe were army leaders—each leading somewhere between a hundred and a thousand men.
\v 15 They crossed to the west side of the Jordan \add river\add* during the spring, at the time of the year when the river floods. They drove out all the people who lived in the valleys on both sides \add of the river\add*.
\p
\v 16 \add Some other men\add* from the tribes of Benjamin and Judah also went to David in his fortress.
\v 17 David went out \add of the cave\add* to meet them, and he told them: “If you’ve come to me in peace, to help me, then I’ll be happy to cooperate with you \add men\add*. But if \add you men plan\add* to betray me to my enemies even though I’ve done you no harm, may the god of our ancestors see \add your motives\add* and judge.”
\p
\v 18 Then \add the\add* spirit empowered Amasay, the head of the thirty, \add and he said\add*: “To you, David, and with you, son of Jesse: peace. Peace to you and peace to the one who helps you, because your god has helped you.” And David received them and appointed them as the heads of the troop.
\p
\v 19 \add Some men\add* from Manasseh joined David when he accompanied the Philistines to battle against \add King\add* Saul. (But David and his men didn’t help the Philistines, because the Philistines’ governors were advised to send them away, as there was a worry that David would feel loyal to his former master, and hence turn on them.)
\v 20 As David was going to Tsiklag, the following men from Manasseh joined him: Adnah, Yozavad, Yedieael, Michael, \add another\add* Yozavad, Elihu, and Tsiletay—the captains of \add units of\add* thousands from Manasseh.
\v 21 They helped David \add stand\add* against bands of raiders, because they were all powerful warriors and military leaders.
\v 22 Every day more men joined David to help him, until his army became very large, like the Israelite people group itself.
\p
\v 23 The following are the numbers of the leaders of those equipped for war. They came to David at Hebron to help him obtain Saul’s kingdom as Yahweh had promised.
\li1
\v 24 From Judah: 6,800 \add warriors\add* ready for battle with shields and spears.
\li1
\v 25 From Simeon: 7,100 powerful \add warriors\add* ready for battle.
\li1
\v 26 From Levi: 4,600.
\li1
\v 27 Yehoyada (a leader \add descended\add* from Aaron brought 3,700 with him.
\li1
\v 28 Zadok, a strong young man, \add brought\add* twenty-two leaders from his clan.
\li1
\v 29 From Benjamin (Saul’s tribe): 3,000 (most of whom had previously served \add King\add* Saul).
\li1
\v 30 From Ephraim: 20,800 powerful warriors who were admired within their own clans.
\li1
\v 31 From the Manasseh half-tribe: 18,000 who were known by name came to make David king.
\li1
\v 32 From Issachar: 200 leaders (with a good understanding of the times and the right thing to do) along with their relatives that they commanded.
\li1
\v 33 From Zebulun: 50,000 experienced warriors with each kind of weapon, and stoic in attitude, yet loyal.
\li1
\v 34 From Naphtali: 1,000 officers, along with 37,000 men carrying shields and spears.
\li1
\v 35 From Dan: 28,600 men prepared for battle.
\li1
\v 36 From Asher: 40,000 \add warriors\add* prepared for battle.
\li1
\v 37 From Reuben, Gad, and the Manasseh half-tribe across the Jordan \add river\add*: 120,000 men armed with all kinds of weapons.
\p
\v 38 All those enthusiastic and capable warriors came to Hebron to make David king over all of Israel. The rest of Israel was also united in wanting to make David king.
\v 39 They were there with David three days, eating and drinking, because their families had sent provisions with them.
\v 40 Also, people who lived near that area (\add even\add* as far as \add the tribal areas belonging to\add* Issachar, Zebulun, and Naphtali were bringing bread on donkeys and camels, and on mules and on oxen: plentiful supplies of flour, fig cakes, raisin cakes, wine, and oil, as well as oxen and sheep, because Israel was celebrating.
\c 13
\s1 Moving the box containing the agreement
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 6:1-11)
\p
\v 1 One day David consulted with all his leaders—the commanders of thousands and of hundreds.
\v 2 David said to the whole Israelite assembly, “If it \add sounds\add* good to you, and to our god Yahweh, let’s spread out to all our different regions to recruit our relatives, along with the priests and the Levites in the cities among their pasturelands, so they can join us.
\v 3 And let’s return our god’s box back here, because we didn’t use it to ask God for guidance in Saul’s time.”
\v 4 All the assembled people agreed because everyone could see that it was the right thing to do.
\p
\v 5 So David assembled all of Israel, from \add everywhere from\add* the Shihor \add river down\add* in Egypt to as far \add north\add* as Levo-Hamat, to bring in God’s box from Kiryat-Yearim.
\v 6 Then David and all of Israel went to Kiryat-Yearim (\add also known as\add* Ba’alah, in Judah, to bring up from there the box of God Yahweh who sits over the winged creatures. \add The box is used when\add* calling his name \add to request help\add*.
\v 7 They transported God’s box on a new cart from Avinadav’s house, and Uza and Ahyo were leading the cart,
\v 8 and David and all of Israel were celebrating in front of God with all their energy, with songs, and with lyres, harps, tambourines, cymbals, and trumpets.
\p
\v 9 \add However,\add* just as they were passing Kidon’s threshing floor, the oxen stumbled and Uza reached out to steady the box.
\v 10 \add That made\add* Yahweh very angry with Uza, and he struck him down because he’d reached out to touch the box, and he died \add right\add* there in front of God.
\v 11 \add That in turn made\add* David angry because Yahweh had burst out against Uza, and he named that place Perez-Uza \add (which means ‘outburst against Uza’), which it’s still called,\add* to this day.
\p
\v 12 David was afraid of God that day and asked himself, “How can I \add possibly\add* bring God’s box to where I am \add in Jerusalem\add*?”
\v 13 So David gave up on bringing the box to where he was in David’s city, and \add instead\add* he took it into the \add nearby\add* house belonging to Obed-Edom the Giti.
\v 14 So God’s box stayed at Obed-Edom’s house for three months, and Yahweh blessed Obed-Edom’s home and everything that belonged to him.
\c 14
\s1 David ‘expansion’ in Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 2:11-16)
\p
\v 1 One day King Huram from Tyre sent messengers to David, along with cedar timber and stone masons and carpenters to build a house for him.
\v 2 \add By then\add* David had realised that Yahweh had established him as king over Israel—that his kingdom was being made more prominent for the sake of Yahweh’s people Israel.
\p
\v 3 Then David took more wives in Jerusalem and he fathered more sons and daughters.
\v 4 These are his children who were born \add there\add* in Jerusalem: Shamua, Shovav, Nathan, Solomon,
\v 5 Yavehar, Elishua, Elifelet,
\v 6 Nogah, Nefeg, Yafia,
\v 7 Elishama, Be’elyada, and Elifelet.
\s1 The defeat of the Philistines
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 5:17-25)
\p
\v 8 When the Philistines heard that David had been anointed as king over all of Israel, the entire Philistine \add army\add* went up \add into the hill country\add* to eliminate him. David got that news and took his army out to meet them.
\v 9 Meanwhile, the Philistines went and raided the Refaim valley,
\v 10 and David asked God, “Should I take on the Philistines, and will you give me victory over them?”
\p “Go ahead,” Yahweh answered, “and I’ll enable you to defeat them.”
\p
\v 11 Then they\f + \fr 14:11 \ft It’s not clear who’s being referred to here.\f* went up to \add what’s now called\add* Baal-Peratsim, and David defeated them down there. David said, “God has used me to break through my enemies like a flooded river breaking a dam.” Therefore they named that place Baal-Peratsim \add (which means ‘the master bursts through’)\add*.
\v 12 The Philistines abandoned their idols \add as they fled\add*, and David ordered them to be burnt.
\p
\v 13 But \add soon\add* the Philistines raided the valley again,
\v 14 and David asked God’s \add advice\add* again. “Don’t go uphill after them,” God said, “\add but\add* circle around them and confront them in front of the balsam trees.
\v 15 When you hear the sound of the marching in the tops of the balsam trees, that’s when you should attack, because God will have gone out ahead of you to strike down the Philistine army.”
\v 16 So David did just as God had told him, and he attacked the Philistines from Gibeon all the way to Gezer.
\v 17 So David’s reputation spread everywhere, and Yahweh caused the other nations to be afraid of him.
\c 15
\s1 Moving the box containing the agreement
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 David constructed houses for his \add wives\add* in David’s city. He \add also\add* prepared a place for God’s \add sacred\add* box and erected a tent for it.
\v 2 Then David ordered, “Only the Levites are allowed to carry God’s box, because Yahweh chose them to carry the box and to minister for him forever.”
\v 3 David assembled all of Israel in Jerusalem to bring Yahweh’s box uphill there to the place that he’d prepared for it.
\v 4 Then he summoned Aaron’s descendants and these Levites:
\li1
\v 5 From Kohat’s descendants \add (Levi’s second son)\add*: Uriel the leader and 120 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 6 From Merari’s descendants \add (Levi’s third son)\add*: Asayah the leader and 220 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 7 From Gereshon’s descendants \add (Levi’s eldest son)\add*: Joel the leader and 130 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 8 From Elitsafan’s descendants: Shemayah the leader and 200 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 9 From Hebron’s descendants: Eliel the leader and eighty of his relatives.
\li1
\v 10 From Uziel’s descendants: Amminadab the leader and 112 of his relatives.
\p
\v 11 David summoned the priests Zadok and Abiathar and these Levites: Uriel, Asayah, Joel, Shemayah, Eliel, and Amminadab.
\v 12 He told them, “You are the heads of the Levite clans. Consecrate yourselves, you and your relatives, and bring Israel’s god Yahweh’s box up to where I’ve prepared \add a place\add* for it.
\v 13 The first \add time we tried,\add* it wasn’t you \add Levites, so\add* our god Yahweh burst out against us because we hadn’t requested his instruction.”
\p
\v 14 So the priests and the Levites consecrated themselves to bring up Israel’s god Yahweh’s \add sacred\add* box.
\v 15 Then, following Yahweh’s instructions as given to Moses, Levi’s descendants carried God’s \add sacred\add* box with its poles on their shoulders.
\p
\v 16 Meanwhile, David instructed the Levite leaders to assign their brothers as the singers, with accompanying instruments—harps, lyres, and cymbals—playing to raise a sound of celebration.
\v 17 The Levites assigned Joel’s son Heyman and his relative Asaf (son of Berechiah) and Eytan (Kushayah’s son, a descendant of Merari),
\v 18 along with their \add Levite\add* cousins: Zechariah, Ben, Ya’aziel, Shemiramot, Yehiel, Uni, Eliav, Benayah, Ma’aseyah, Matityah, Elifelehu, Mikneyah, and two of the \add temple\add* gatekeepers, Obed-Edom and Yeiel.
\v 19 Heyman, Asaf, and Eytan sang and also played bronze cymbals.
\v 20 Zechariah, Aziel, Shemiramot, Yehiel, Uni, Eliav, Ma’aseyah, and Benayah played harps in a certain musical style.
\v 21 Matityah, Elifelehu, Mikneyah, Obed-Edom, Yeiel, and Azazyah played lyres in a different musical style.
\p
\v 22 Kenanyah, the leader of the musical Levites, directed the singing because he was very capable.
\v 23 Berechiah and Elkanah \add were two of\add* the men who guarded the \add sacred\add* box.
\v 24 The priests Shevanyah, Yoshafat, Netanel, Amasay, Zechariah, Benayah, and Eliezer were the ones who blew trumpets in front of God’s \add sacred\add* box. Obed-Edom and Yehiyah \add also\add* guarded the \add sacred\add* box.
\p
\v 25 So David, the Israelite elders, and the top army commanders went to \add get\add* the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement from Obed-Edom’s house with \add much\add* celebration.
\v 26 Indeed, God helped the Levites carrying the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement, and they slaughtered seven bulls and seven rams.
\v 27 David was wearing a fine-linen robe, and so were all the Levites carrying the box, and the singers, and the choir director Kenanyah. In addition, David \add wore\add* a linen chestpiece.
\v 28 So all of Israel brought the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement up \add the hill\add* with happy shouting, and with a sound of a \add ram’s\add* horn, and with trumpets and cymbals, \add along with the sound of\add* harps and lyres.
\p
\v 29 As the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement was being brought inside David’s city, Michal (Saul’s daughter) looked out the window and saw \add her husband,\add* King David, dancing and celebrating, and she secretly despised him.
\c 16
\s1 The sacred box is placed in the tent
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then they brought in God’s box and put it in the middle of the tent that David had erected for it, and they offered burnt offerings and peace offerings in front of God.
\v 2 When David finished presenting the burnt offering and the peace offerings, he blessed the people in Yahweh’s name.
\v 3 Then he distributed a loaf of bread, a date cake, and a raisin cake to each man and woman in Israel.
\p
\v 4 Then David appointed some Levites to minister in front of Yahweh’s box and to commemorate and to thank and to praise Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 5 Asaf was their leader, with Zechariah as his assistant. The other Levites who helped were Yeiel, Shemiramot, Yehiel, Matityah, Eliav, Benayah, Obed-Edom, and Yeiel. They played harps and lyres, and Asaf played cymbals.
\v 6 Benayah and Yahaziel the priests regularly \add blew\add* trumpets in front of the box \add containing\add* God’s agreement.
\s1 A praise song
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (Psa. 105:1-15; 96:1-3; 106:1,47-48)
\p
\v 7 On that day David first gave Asaf and his helpers this song to praise Yahweh:
\q1
\v 8 Give thanks to Yahweh \add and\add* use his name when calling out \add for help\add*.
\q1 →Tell all the other nations about what he’s done \add for you Israelites\add*.
\q1
\v 9 Sing to him \add and\add* make music to him.
\q1 →Tell \add everyone\add* about all the wonderful things that he’s done.
\q1
\v 10 Boast about his untarnished reputation.
\q1 →Those who follow Yahweh should celebrate.
\q1
\v 11 Ask Yahweh to help you and give you his strength.
\q1 Constantly ask for his direction.
\q1
\v 12 Remember the wonderful things that he’s done—
\q1 his miracles and how he punished those who deserved it.
\q1
\v 13 You descendants of his servant Israel,
\q1 you descendants of Jacob, the one who he chose,
\q1
\v 14 he is our god, Yahweh.
\q1 He judges the entire world.
\q1
\v 15 Remember his agreement forever—
\q1 the instructions that he gave to a thousand generations,
\q1
\v 16 the agreement that he made with Abraham,
\q1 and his promise to Isaac.
\q1
\v 17 He established it as a law for Jacob—
\q1 as an eternal agreement for Israel,
\q1
\v 18 saying, “I’ll give the Canaan region to you—
\q1 your portion that will then be your inheritance.”
\b
\q1
\v 19 When your ancestors were still a small group,
\q1 insignificant, and strangers in the land,
\q1
\v 20 and they wandered back and forth from nation to nation,
\q1 and from one kingdom to another,
\q2
\v 21 God didn’t permit anyone to oppress them,
\q1 and he rebuked kings on their behalf:
\q1
\v 22 “Don’t attack my chosen people,
\q1 and don’t harm my prophets.”
\b
\q1
\v 23 Sing to Yahweh everybody.
\q1 →Every day tell others that he’s saved us.
\q1
\v 24 Tell the other nations about his power.
\q1 Tell all the people groups about the wonderful things he’s done,
\q1
\v 25 because Yahweh is great and deserves a lot of praise,
\q1 and he should be revered more than any other god.
\q1
\v 26 All those gods of the \add other\add* people groups are worthless,
\q1 ^but it was Yahweh who created the heavens.
\q1
\v 27 His presence emanates splendour and grandeur,
\q1 \add and\add* strength and happiness \add radiate\add* from his place.
\b
\q1
\v 28 You people in nations all over the world, praise Yahweh—
\q1 praise him for his splendour and his strength.
\q1
\v 29 Praise Yahweh for his great reputation.
\q1 Bring an offering and come into his presence.
\q1 Worship Yahweh in \add his\add* splendour and purity.
\q1
\v 30 Let the entire planet tremble if he comes.
\q1 The world is firmly established—it can’t be shaken \add around\add*.
\q1
\v 31 Let the heavens be glad, and let the earth shout in celebration,
\q1 and let people in all the nations say, “Yahweh is king.”
\q1
\v 32 Let the oceans roar along with all the creatures in them.
\q1 Let the countryside celebrate along with all the creatures that live there.
\q1
\v 33 Then the trees in the forests should shout happily when Yahweh comes,
\q1 because he is coming to judge the world.
\b
\q1
\v 34 Give thanks to Yahweh, because he’s good,
\q1 because his loyal commitment \add lasts\add* forever.
\q1
\v 35 Then say, “Rescue us, God who’s our saviour,
\q1 and bring us together and save us from \add attacks\add* by other nations,
\q1 →so that we’ll thank you for your untarnished reputation,
\q1 and boast about your praiseworthy actions.”
\q1
\v 36 Blessed be Israel’s god Yahweh,
\q1 in eternity past and eternity to come.
\q1 And all the people said, “Amen” and praised Yahweh.
\s1 Worshipping in Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 37 Then David went away from where Yahweh’s box had been placed. Asaf and his brothers were left to minister in front of the box continually—in the appropriate way each day depending on the occasion—
\v 38 along with Obed-Edom and sixty-eight relatives. Obed-Edom (Yedutun’s son) and Hosah were gatekeepers.
\p
\v 39 Also, Zadok the priest and his relatives the priests were at the front of Yahweh’s tabernacle at the high place, which was in Gibeon,
\v 40 to continually present burnt offerings to Yahweh on the altar for burnt offerings, morning and evening, following all the instructions Yahweh had given Israel , which had been written down.
\v 41 With them were Heyman and Yedutun and the rest of those chosen, who were designated by name to thank Yahweh because his loyal commitment \add lasts\add* forever.
\v 42 Heyman and Yedutun had brought trumpets and cymbals to accompany the songs for God, and Yedutun’s sons at the gate \add joined in also\add*.
\p
\v 43 Then all the people left and went home, and David \add also\add* returned \add home\add* to bless his household.
\c 17
\s1 Nathan’s message for David
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 7:1-17)
\p
\v 1 After a while, when David was living in his house, he said to the prophet Nathan, “Look, \add here\add* I am living in a palace \add made\add* of cedar, but the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement is \add kept\add* in a tent.”
\v 2 “Go and do what you’re planning,” Nathan replied, “because God is with you.”
\p
\v 3 But that night God spoke to Nathan,
\v 4 “Go and tell my servant David that Yahweh says this: You yourself won’t be the one to build a residence for me to live in,
\v 5 because I haven’t lived in a building from the day that I brought Israel \add out of Egypt\add* until today—I’ve moved from place to place and tent to tent.
\v 6 As I travelled with all the Israelites, did I ever complain to any of Israel’s leaders who I’d commanded to shepherd my people, and ask them, ‘Why haven’t you \add people\add* built a house out of cedar for me?’ ”
\p
\v 7 “So now you should tell my servant David that Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: I myself took you from the pasture, from behind the sheep, to be a leader over my people, Israel.
\v 8 I’ve always been with you wherever you’ve gone, and I’ve defeated all your enemies who were attacking you, and I’ll make you famous like some of the greatest people in the world.
\v 9 I’ll choose a place for my people, Israel, and I’ll plant them, and they’ll settle in that place and not need to be fearful again. Violent groups won’t continue to trouble them like they did in the past,
\v 10 ever since I first put heroes and leaders over my people, Israel. Then I’ll humble all your enemies. In addition, I declare to you that Yahweh will build a dynasty for you.
\v 11 When your time on earth is up, and it’s time to join your deceased ancestors, then I’ll raise up one of your sons and establish his kingdom.
\v 12 He’ll be the one to build a temple for me and establish a dynasty that will last forever.
\v 13 I myself will be a father to him, and he’ll be a son to me, and I won’t cease my loyal commitment to him like I ended it with your predecessor.
\v 14 I’ll confirm his rule over my temple and my kingdom forever, and his ruling dynasty will last forever.”
\p
\v 15 So Nathan passed all of that on to David.
\s1 David praises Yahweh
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 7:18-29)
\p
\v 16 Then king David went in and sat in front of Yahweh, and asked, “Who am I, Yahweh God, and what is my family, that you’ve brought me to this point?
\v 17 This was such a small thing in your eyes, God, and you’ve spoken about the future of your servant’s family, and you’ve blessed me more than I deserve, Yahweh God.
\v 18 What more can I, David, say to you \add about how\add* you’ve honoured your servant? You certainly know me well.
\v 19 Yahweh, you’ve done all these great things for the sake of your servant, and done just what you wanted in order to reveal your greatness.
\v 20 Yahweh, there’s no one else like you, and no god except you, as we’ve all heard for ourselves.
\v 21 There’s no other country like your people, Israel—the only nation in the world where \add you\add*, God, rescued the people in order to make them your own. That made you famous, and also feared, as you drove the people groups out ahead of your people who you ransomed out of Egypt.
\v 22 You took your people, Israel, to be your people forever, and you, Yahweh, became our god.
\p
\v 23 “And now, Yahweh, may what you said about your servant and his family be confirmed forever, and come to pass just like you’ve said.
\v 24 May your reputation be established and increase forever, as \add people\add* say, ‘Commander-in-chief Yahweh, Israel’s god, is god over Israel.’ And your servant David’s family has been established as you’ve watched,
\v 25 because you, God, have revealed to your servant that you’ll give me a dynasty. That’s why I’m \add brave enough\add* to pray to you.
\v 26 Yes, Yahweh, you are God and you’ve promised these good things to \add me,\add* your servant.
\v 27 And you’ve now decided to bless your servant’s family to be in front of you forever, because you, Yahweh, have blessed \add us\add*, and so \add we’ll\add* be blessed forever.
\c 18
\s1 David’s victories
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 8:1-18)
\p
\v 1 Sometime afterwards, David attacked and defeated the Philistines, subduing them, and he captured the Philistine city of Gath and its surrounding villages.
\v 2 Then he attacked and defeated Moab, and the Moabites became servants to David—taking him \add regular\add* tribute.
\p
\v 3 Next David attacked and defeated King Hadadezer of Tsovah-Hamat, when that king had tried enforce his control at the Euphrates \add river\add*.
\v 4 David captured 1,000 chariots, 7,000 horsemen, and 20,000 foot-soldiers from him. And David hamstrung all but one hundred of the chariot \add horses\add*.
\p
\v 5 When the Syrians \add (or, Syrians\add* \add )\add* came from Damascus to help King Hadadezer, David killed 22,000 of them.
\v 6 Then David established forces in the Aramean \add town\add* of Damascus, and the Syrians became servants to David, taking him \add regular\add* tribute. So Yahweh helped David wherever he went.
\v 7 David took the gold shields that were used by Hadadezer’s servants and brought them to Jerusalem.
\v 8 He also took a lot of bronze from Hadadezer’s cities of Tivhat and Kun. (\add Later,\add* Solomon used it to make the huge bronze basin called ‘the sea’, along with its pillars and other bronze items.)
\p
\v 9 When King Toeu from Hamat heard that David had defeated King Hadadezer’s entire army from Tsovah,
\v 10 he sent his son Hadoram to King David carrying various items of gold, silver, and bronze. \add Hadoram went\add* to ask David for peace and to bless him because he’d fought against Hadadezer and struck him down, because Toeu had \add also\add* been at war with Hadadezer.
\v 11 King David set that apart as holy to Yahweh, along with the gold and silver that he’d brought back from other nations: from Edom, from Moab, from the Ammonites, from the Philistines, and from the Amalekites.
\p
\v 12 Avishay (whose \add mother\add* was Tseruyah) defeated Edom in the Salt valley, \add killing\add* eighteen thousand \add of them\add*.
\v 13 Then David stationed garrisons in Edom, and all of Edom became his servants. And Yahweh saved David wherever he went.
\p
\v 14 So David ruled over all of Israel and administered justice and did what was right for all his people.
\v 15 Tseruyah’s son Joab led the army, and Ahilud’s son Jehoshaphat was the record keeper.
\v 16 Ahituv’s son Zadok and Abiathar’s son Ahimelek were priests, and Shavsha was the secretary.
\v 17 Yehoyada’s son Benayah supervised the Keretim and Peletim \add who were David’s bodyguards\add*, and David’s sons were his leading officials.
\c 19
\s1 The defeat of the Ammonites and Syrians
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 10:1-19)
\p
\v 1 Some time later, the Ammonite King Nahash died and his son \add Hanun\add* became their king,
\v 2 and David said, “I’ll show loyal commitment to Nahash’s son Hanun, because his father was good to me.” So David sent messengers to comfort him concerning his father’s \add death\add*. However, when David’s servants entered Ammonite territory to comfort Hanun,
\v 3 the Ammonite leaders asked their new king, “Do you think that David’s \add really\add* sending comforters to honour your father? Wouldn’t it be more likely that he sent his servants to explore and spy out the land in order to overthrow you?”
\v 4 So \add King\add* Hanun took David’s servants and \add had their beards\add* shaved off, plus he cut off the bottom half of their robes to expose their buttocks, and then he sent them home.
\v 5 Some people quickly informed David what had happened, so he sent \add messengers\add* to meet the men (because they were very much humiliated) and to tell them, “Stay \add there\add* in Jericho until your beards grow \add out\add*, then return \add here\add*.”
\p
\v 6 By then, the Ammonites realised that they’d greatly insulted \add King\add* David, so \add King\add* Hanun and the Ammonites sent thirty tonnes of silver to hire chariots and horsemen from Aram-Naharayim, Aram-Ma’akah, and Tsovah.
\v 7 They hired 32,000 chariots, as well as the king of Ma’akah and his army. They \add all\add* came and set up their camps near Meydeva, plus they were joined by the Ammonites \add themselves\add* coming out of their cities ready for battle.
\p
\v 8 When David heard \add about that\add*, he sent Joab with the entire army.
\v 9 Then the Ammonites marched out and deployed ready for battle at the entrance to their city, while the kings who’d come were over in the countryside \add with their men\add*.
\p
\v 10 Joab \add quickly\add* realised that he’d have to fight on two fronts, so he chose Israel’s best warriors and sent them out to face the Syrians,
\v 11 while he sent the remaining men (under the control of his \add older brother\add* Avishay) out against the Ammonites.
\v 12 Joab told them, “If the Syrians are too strong for me, then you can come to rescue me. But if the Ammonites start to gain on you, then I’ll \add come to\add* rescue you.
\v 13 We must be strong and fight hard to defend our people and our cities that belong to our god. Yahweh will do what he considers to be good.”
\p
\v 14 So Joab advanced his troops to attack the Syrians, but the Syrians fled away from them.
\v 15 When the Ammonites saw that the Syrians had fled, they also fled from Avishay and withdrew into the city, so Joab went back to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 16 When the Syrians saw that they were defeated by Israel, they sent messengers to bring more Syrians from across the Euphrates river, led by Shofak, the commander of Hadadezer’s army.
\v 17 When that was reported to David, he gathered all of Israel and crossed the Jordan. They positioned themselves against the Syrians, who then fought against them.
\v 18 But the Syrians fled away from the Israelites, and David killed seven thousand chariot \add drivers\add* and forty thousand men on foot, as well as killing Shofak, their army commander.
\v 19 When Hadadezer’s servants saw that Israel had defeated them, they made peace with David and became his subjects, so Syria wasn’t willing to help the Ammonites \add militarily\add* any more.
\c 20
\s1 David captures Rabah
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 12:26-31)
\p
\v 1 Then, in the spring, when kings often march out to war, Joab led the army out for war, and he devastated the Ammonites land. Then he reached \add the capital,\add* Rabah, and he besieged it while David stayed in Jerusalem. Joab defeated Rabah and demolished the city.
\v 2 Then David took their king’s crown off his head—it was gold with a valuable gemstone set in it, and weighed thirty kilograms. He put the crown on his own head, plus he took a very large amount of plunder out of the city.
\v 3 Then they took all the people out of the city and forced them to work with saws, iron picks, and axes. David did that to the people from all the Ammonite cities, then he returned to Jerusalem with his army.
\s1 Battles against Gitim
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 21:15-22)
\p
\v 4 Some time later, war broke out with the Philistines at Gezer, then Sibkay the Hushati struck down Sipay, from the descendants of the Refaim, and they were subdued.
\p
\v 5 Then there was another battle with the Philistines, and Yair’s son Elhanan killed Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Giti \add (that is, from Gath)\add*, even though his spear shaft was \add as thick\add* as the beam of a weaver’s \add loom\add*.
\v 6 There was also war at Gath, where there was a very tall man with six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot, who was also a descendant of Rafa.
\v 7 When he insulted Israel, Shimea’s son Jonathan killed him. (Shimea was one of David’s \add older\add* brothers.)
\p
\v 8 Those were the descendants of Rafa from Gath who were killed by David and his men.
\c 21
\s1 David orders a census
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 24:1-25)
\s2 Joab doesn’t want to do the census
\p
\v 1 Then a trouble-maker worked against Israel by encouraging David to take a census of Israel,
\v 2 so David ordered Joab and the other officials, “Go \add and\add* count Israel from Beersheba \add in the south to all the way\add* as far \add north\add* as Dan, then come and tell me, so that I’ll know how many warriors I have.”
\p
\v 3 “My master, the king, may Yahweh multiply his people one hundred times over. Aren’t they all your servants?” Joab \add diplomatically\add* queried, “\add So\add* why does my master want to do that? Why should he bring judgement on Israel?”
\v 4 But the king insisted, so Joab was forced to go and gather statistics on the entire country. Then he returned to Jerusalem
\v 5 and reported the numbers to David. Israel had 1.1 million sword-wielding men, and Judah had 470 thousand.
\v 6 However, Joab had \add intentionally\add* not counted the Levi and Benjamin \add tribes\add*, because he was horrified by the king’s command.
\s2 Punishment for the census
\p
\v 7 God was displeased by what David had ordered, and he punished Israel,
\v 8 and David said to God, “I’ve disobeyed you badly by doing that. Please forgive your servant for his disobedience, because I’ve been very stupid.”
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told David’s prophet Gad,
\v 10 “Go and tell David that Yahweh says this: I’ll give you three options—choose one of them and that’s what I’ll do to you.”
\p
\v 11 So Gad went to David and told him, “Yahweh says that you must choose \add one of these punishments\add*:
\v 12 either three years of famine, or three months of being overcome by your enemies’ armies, or three days of Yahweh’s ‘sword’ of pestilence in the country and Yahweh’s messenger bringing destruction across all of Israel’s territory. So now, tell me what answer I should take back to the one who sent me.”
\p
\v 13 “\add Oh,\add* that’s very distressing,” David replied to Gad, “but please don’t let me fall into my enemies’ hands. Yahweh is very merciful, so please let him be the one to punish me directly.”
\p
\v 14 So Yahweh sent a disease throughout Israel, and seventy thousand Israelite men died.
\v 15 Then God sent a messenger to destroy Jerusalem, and while the disease was still spreading, Yahweh looked down and relented about the tragedy, and he told the destroying messenger, “Enough! Now stop what you’re doing.” Now Yahweh’s messenger \add happened to be\add* standing at the threshing floor belonging to Ornan the Jebusite.
\p
\v 16 \add As\add* David looked up, he saw Yahweh’s messenger standing between the earth and the heavens, holding his sword stretched out over Jerusalem. Then David and the elders, who were \add all\add* dressed in \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth, fell to their knees and bowed their faces to the ground.
\v 17 Then David asked God, “Wasn’t it me myself who said to count the people? It’s me who disobeyed and clearly did what was wrong, but these sheep, what have they done? Yahweh my god, please direct your punishment against me and my extended family, but don’t continue this deadly disease on your people.”
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh’s messenger told Gad to tell David that he should build an altar to Yahweh at Ornan the Jebusite’s threshing floor.
\v 19 So David went up \add to that place when he heard\add* Gad’s message that was delivered in Yahweh’s name.
\v 20 Meanwhile, Ornan was threshing wheat, and he turned and saw Yahweh’s messenger, and he and his four sons who were with him \add quickly\add* hid behind something.
\v 21 When David arrived there, Ornan saw him and left the threshing floor and knelt in front of David, bowing his face to the ground.
\v 22 Then David asked Ornan, “Give me this place with its threshing floor, and I’ll build an altar to Yahweh on it. Sell it to me at the full price, so the deadly disease affecting the people will be stopped.”
\p
\v 23 “My master the king, take anything you want,” Ornan replied. “Listen, I’ll donate the cattle for the burnt offerings, and the threshing sledges for firewood, and the wheat for the offering—all at no charge.”
\p
\v 24 “No, I’ll most definitely pay the full price,” King David responded. “I couldn’t offer a burnt offering to Yahweh if it hadn’t cost me anything.”
\v 25 So David paid Ornan six hundred gold pieces for his place,
\v 26 and he built an altar to Yahweh there and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings on it. He called to Yahweh, and Yahweh answered him with fire from the heavens on that altar with the burnt offering.
\p
\v 27 Then Yahweh spoke to his messenger, who then returned his sword to his sheath.
\s1 The location for the future temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 28 When David saw that Yahweh had answered him at Ornan the Jebusite’s threshing floor, he sacrificed there.
\v 29 At that time, Yahweh’s sacred tent that Moses had made in the wilderness, and the altar for burnt sacrifices, were on a hill used for worship near Gibeon \add city\add*.
\v 30 David wasn’t able to go there to ask God’s direction because he was terrified of Yahweh’s messenger’s sword.
\c 22
\p
\v 1 Then David said, “This is \add now\add* the residence of Yahweh God, and this is the altar for Israel’s burnt offering.”
\s1 The preparation of standing up of temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 2 Then David said to gather the foreigners who lived in Israel, and he assigned them the job of hewing out cut stones to build the residence for God.
\v 3 He prepared a large quantity of iron for the door hinges and fittings, so much brass that it couldn’t be weighed,
\v 4 and innumerable cedar logs that the Sidonians and the Tyrians brought in for David.
\v 5 David said, “My son Solomon is young and not capable, and the house to be built for Yahweh must be impressive for the sake of his reputation and honour everywhere, \add so\add* I’ll prepare for it now.” So David did a lot of preparation before he died.
\p
\v 6 Then David called for his son Solomon, and ordered him to build a residence for Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 7 David told him, “My son, I wanted to build a temple to honour my god Yahweh,
\v 8 but Yahweh gave me this message, ‘You’ve fought a lot of battles and shed a large amount of blood, \add so\add* you won’t be the one who’ll build a residence to honour me—you’ve shed too much blood on the ground in my sight.’
\v 9 \add He told me,\add* ‘Listen, you’ll have a son who’ll be a man of peace. I’ll give him relief from all his enemies from the surrounding regions. That’s why he’ll be called ‘Solomon’ \add (which sounds like ‘Shalom’—the Hebrew word for peace)\add*, and I’ll give peace and quietness to Israel during his lifetime.
\v 10 He’ll be the one to build a residence for me, and he himself will become my son, and I’ll become his father, and I’ll establish his kingdom’s authority over Israel forever.’
\p
\v 11 “Now, my son, may Yahweh be with you, and may you be successful and build a residence for Yahweh your god, just as he said you would.
\v 12 I also ask Yahweh to give you insight and understanding, and give you charge over Israel so that you can keep your god Yahweh’s regulations.
\v 13 Then you’ll succeed, if you’re careful to follow the instructions and the directions that Yahweh commanded Moses concerning Israel. Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid or discouraged.
\p
\v 14 Now, see, despite many battles, I’ve prepared the following for Yahweh’s residence: three thousand tonnes of gold and thirty thousand tonnes of silver, along with too much bronze and iron to weigh, and timber and \add cut\add* stones. Plus you’ll be able to add more.
\v 15 You’ll have plenty of skilled workers, including those who work with stone and those who work with wood.
\v 16 Then there’s so many skilled with gold and silver, and bronze and iron. So get organised and get started, and may Yahweh be with you.
\p
\v 17 Then David ordered all the Israelite officials to assist his son Solomon, \add saying\add*,
\v 18 “Isn’t Yahweh your god with you \add officials\add*, and hasn’t he given you peace on every side? He’s allowed me to take control of this region, and this area has been subdued with the help of Yahweh and his people.
\v 19 Now, dedicate your minds and strength to requesting advice from your god Yahweh, then get organised and build Yahweh God’s sanctuary so you can bring the box containing Yahweh’s agreement to the residence to be built to honour Yahweh’s name, and bring God’s sacred items.”
\c 23
\p
\v 1 By then David was old and coming to the end of his life, and he made his son Solomon king of Israel.
\s1 The Levite families and their assignments
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 2 Then David gathered all the Israelite officials and the priests and Levites.
\v 3 Counting the Levite males that were thirty years old and older, they found there were thirty-eight thousand men.
\v 4 \add David assigned\add* twenty-four thousand of them to supervise the work at Yahweh’s residence, six thousand to be officials and judges,
\v 5 four thousand to be gatekeepers, and four thousand to praise Yahweh using the musical instruments that he’d provided.
\p
\v 6 So David divided the Levites into divisions according to \add their ancestry through\add* Levi’s sons Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.
\s1 The Gershonim
\p
\v 7 The Gershonim \add included\add* Laedan and Shimei.
\v 8 Laedan’s three sons were: Yehiel (the oldest), Zetam, and Joel.
\v 9 Shimei’s\f + \fr 23:9 \ft Possibly the wrong name got into the Hebrew here, because it disagrees with v10.\f* three sons were: Shelomot, Haziel, and Haran. (They were leaders of Laedan’s clans.)
\v 10 Shimei’s four sons were: Yahat, Zina,\f + \fr 23:10 \ft Some ancient translations have Zizah here, which would seem to fit better with v11.\f* Ye’ush, and Beriah.
\v 11 (Yahat was the head, and Zizah was second. Ye’ush and Beriah were grouped together as one family unit because they didn’t have many descendants.)
\s2 The Kohatites
\p
\v 12 Kohat’s four sons were: Amram, Yitshar, Hebron, and Uziel.
\v 13 Amram’s sons were Aaron and Moses. Aaron and his future male descendants were permanently appointed to consecrate the most sacred things, to burn incense, to minister to Yahweh, and to bless his name.
\v 14 Moses \add was known as\add* a man who served God, and his descendants were included with the \add other\add* Levites.
\v 15 Moses’s sons were Gereshom and Eliezer.
\v 16 Gereshom’s eldest son was Shevuel.
\v 17 Eliezer’s only son was Rehavyah, but Rehavyah \add went on to\add* have many sons.
\p
\v 18 Yitshar’s eldest son was Shelomot.
\v 19 Hebron’s sons from oldest to youngest were: Yeriyah, Amaryah, Yahaziel, and Yekameam.
\v 20 Uziel’s sons were Micah and Yishiyah.
\s2 The Merarim
\p
\v 21 Merari’s sons were Mahli and Mushi, \add and\add* Mahli’s sons were Eleazar and Kish.
\v 22 Eleazar died without having any sons, \add so\add* his brother Kish’s sons married his daughters \add (to continue the family name)\add*.
\v 23 Mushi had three sons: Mahli, Eder, and Yerimot.
\p
\v 24 Those were Levi’s descendants by their clans and their appointments, who did the work of serving in Yahweh’s residence once they reached twenty years old,
\v 25 because David had said, “Israel’s god Yahweh has given peace to his people, and he’ll \add now\add* live forever in Jerusalem,
\v 26 so the Levites won’t need to carry the sacred tent anymore, along with all its equipment.”
\v 27 So one of David’s final instructions had been to count the Levite men who were twenty years old and over,
\v 28 because their work was to assist Aaron’s descendants in their work in Yahweh’s residence. They were in charge of the courtyards and siderooms, in charge of the purification ceremonies, and did other work at God’s residence.
\v 29 They were also in charge of the \add sacred\add* bread, as well as the fine flour for the offering, the wafers of bread without raising agent, the baking pans, and all the measuring and mixing of ingredients.
\p
\v 30 Every morning and every evening the Levites stood and praised Yahweh.
\v 31 They would also do that on the rest days, and at the new moon celebrations and during the other religious festivals. There were regulations about how many of them must always be on duty serving Yahweh.
\v 32 They were \add also\add* in charge of the sacred tent and the holy place, and assisting \add the priests\add* (their relatives, Aaron’s descendants) in their service at Yahweh’s residence.
\c 24
\s1 The Levites’ duties
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Aaron’s sons were: Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar,
\v 2 but Nadav and Avihu died before their father and without having any children, so \add only\add* Eleazar and Itamar became priests.
\v 3 David gave Eleazar’s descendant Zadok, and Itamar’s descendant Ahimelek two different sets of duties to perform.
\v 4 There were more leaders among Eleazar’s descendants than there were among Itamar’s descendants, so they appointed sixteen leaders from Eleazar’s descendants and eight from Itamar’s descendants.
\v 5 They divided up the duties by a process of choosing randomly, so some of the leaders from each of the two groups served the facility and others served God directly. DOUBLE-CHECK, along with verse 3 as well.
\v 6 Netanel’s son Shemayah was the Levite scribe who recorded all that in front of the king and the leaders, Zadok the priest and Abiathar’s son Ahimelek, and the clan leaders of the priests and the Levites. (One family from Eleazar’s group was randomly assigned a duty, and then one from Itamar’s group, and so on.)
\p
\v 7 The first two allocations of duties went to Yehoyariv and then Yedayah.
\v 8 Next were Harim and Se’orim,
\v 9 Malkiyah and Miyamin,
\v 10 Hakots and Abijah,
\v 11 Yeshua and Shekanyah,
\v 12 Elyashiv and Yakim,
\v 13 Hupah and Yeshev’av,
\v 14 Bilgah and Imer,
\v 15 Hezir and Hapitsets,
\v 16 Petahyah and Yehezkel,
\v 17 Yakin and Gemul,
\v 18 Delayah and, lastly, Ma’azyah.
\p
\v 19 Those \add were the priests\add* selected to \add be leaders of the groups\add* that would serve in Yahweh’s temple, following the instructions that Israel’s god Yahweh had given their ancestor Aaron.
\s2 Other Levites
\p
\v 20 This is a list of some of Levi’s other descendants:
\li1 From Amram’s sons: Shuvael.
\li1 From Shuvael’s sons: Yehdiyah.
\li1
\v 21 From Rehavyah’s sons: Yishiyah, the leader.
\li1
\v 22 From the Yitshari: Shelomot.
\li1 From Shelomot’s sons: Yahat.
\li1
\v 23 \add Hebron’s\add*\f + \fr 24:33 \ft Missing here in the Hebrew, but see \xt 1 Chr. 23:19\ft .\f* sons (in order): Yeriyah, Amaryah, Yahaziel, Yekameam
\li1
\v 24 From Uziel’s sons: Micah.
\li1 From Micah’s sons: Shamir.
\li1
\v 25 Micah’s younger brother: Yishiyah.
\li1 From Yishiyah’s sons: Zechariah.
\li1
\v 26 Merari’s sons: Mahli and Mushi.
\li1 Ya’aziyah’s son: Beno.
\li1
\v 27 Merari’s descendants through Ya’aziyah: Beno, Shoham, Zakur, and Ivri.
\li1
\v 28 From Mahli: Eleazar who had no sons.
\li1
\v 29 Kish’s son: Yerahme’el.
\li1
\v 30 Mushi’s sons: Mahli, Eder, and Yerimot.
\p Those were the Levites listed by their ancestors’ families.
\v 31 They, too, used a process of choosing randomly, just like Aaron’s descendants (their relatives), \add to determine their specific duties\add* irrespective of where they came in their families. That was done in front of King David, Zadok and Ahimelek, the clan leaders, and the \add other\add* priests and Levites.
\c 25
\s1 The temple singers
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then David and the army chiefs allocated the \add musical\add* service to the sons of Asaf, Heyman, and Yedutun—the ones prophesying with harps, with lyres, and with cymbals. This is a list of the men they chose for that work:
\li1
\v 2 From Asaf’s sons: Zakur, Joseph, Netanyah, and Asarelah. They were supervised by Asaf, who prophesied under the king’s direction.
\li1
\v 3 Six from Yedutun’s family: Gedalyah, Tseri, Isaiah, Hashavyah, and Matityah. They were supervised by Yedutun, who prophesied with a harp, and thanked and praised Yahweh.
\li1
\v 4 From Heyman’s sons: Bukiyah, Matanyah, Uziel, Shuvael, Yerimot, Hananyah, Hanani, Eliatah, Gidalti, Romamti-Ezer, Yashebekashah, Maloti, Hotir, and Mahaziot.
\v 5 They were all sons of King David’s prophet Heyman, who’d been promised by God that he’d strengthen him, and God gave him fourteen sons and three daughters.
\p
\v 6 All of those served as musicians in Yahweh’s temple under their fathers’ supervision—they played cymbals, harps, and lyres. Their fathers were supervised by the king.
\v 7 There were 288 of those men and their musical relatives who played to Yahweh.
\v 8 They used a process of choosing randomly to determine their duties, whether young or old, and irrespective of their musical abilities.
\p
\v 9 The first allocation went (in order) to:
\li1 Asaf and his son Joseph,
\li1 Gedalyah and his relatives and sons (a total of twelve),
\li1
\v 10 Zakur and his sons and relatives (another twelve),
\li1
\v 11 Yitsri and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 12 Netanyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 13 Bukiyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 14 Yesarelah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 15 Isaiah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 16 Matanyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 17 Shimei and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 18 Azarel and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 19 Hashavyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 20 Shuvael and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 21 Matityah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 22 Yerimot and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 23 Hananyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 24 Yashebekashah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 25 Hanani and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 26 Maloti and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 27 Eliatah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 28 Hotir and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 29 Gidalti and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 30 Mahaziot and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 31 Romamti-Ezer and his sons and relatives (twelve).
\c 26
\s1 The guards of doorway of temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 These were the divisions of the gatekeepers:
\li1 From the Korhim: Kore’s son Meshelemyah (Kore was one of Asaf’s sons),
\li1
\v 2 Meshelemyah’s \add seven\add* sons (in order): Zechariah, Yedieael, Zevadyah, Yatniel,
\v 3 Eylam, Yehohanan, and Elyehoeynay.
\li1
\v 4 Obed-Edom’s \add eight\add* sons (in order): Shemayah, Yehozavad, Yoah, Sakar, Netanel,
\v 5 Amiyel, Issachar, and Pe’uletay. (God had blessed him.)
\li1
\v 6 Obed-Edom’s son Shemayah also had sons. (They were leaders in their father’s family because they were capable warriors.)
\v 7 Shemayah’s sons were: Otni, Refael, Obed, and Elzavad. His relatives Elihu and Semakyah were also capable men.
\p
\v 8 All those descendants of Obed-Edom and their sons and relatives were capable people and strong workers. Altogether there were 62 of them.
\li1
\v 9 Meshelemyah had sons and relatives who were respected (eighteen in total).
\li1
\v 10 Merari’s descendant Hosah’s sons: Shimri (not \add actually\add* the firstborn, but his father appointed him as the first),
\v 11 Hilkiyah, Tevalyah, and Zechariah. (Thirteen sons and relatives in total.)
\p
\v 12 Those men were leaders of the groups of men who guarded the temple’s gates. They worked in Yahweh’s temple like their relatives did.
\v 13 They used a process of choosing randomly to assign each family to a particular gate, irrespective of the family reputation.
\v 14 Shelemyah’s group was allocated to the eastern gate, and his son Zechariah (a wise adviser) was allocated to the northern gate.
\v 15 Obed-Edom’s group was allocated to the southern gate, plus his sons \add were to guard\add* the storehouses.
\v 16 Shupim’s and Hosah’s groups were allocated the western gate, along with the Shaleket gate on the uphill road, with side-by-side guards.
\v 17 Every day there were six Levites on duty in the east, four in the north, four in the south, and two at the storehouses.
\v 18 There were four on the road by the western courtyard, and two in the courtyard.
\v 19 Those were the groups of men who were Korah’s and Merari’s descendants who guarded the \add temple\add* gates.
\s1 The guards of things/objects
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 20 The Levite Ahiyah was in charge of the temple treasuries, as well as the storehouses for dedicated gifts.
\v 21 Laedan’s descendants (\add also\add* descendants of Gereshon) were leaders among their family groups. Yehiel was a descendant of that Laedan.
\v 22 Yehiel’s sons were Zetam and Joel, \add and they\add* were in charge of the storehouses in Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 23 \add Others who did that work\add* were descendants of Amram, Yitshar, Hebron, and Uziel.
\p
\v 24 Shuvael, a descendant of Moses’ son Gereshom, was \add another\add* leader in charge of the treasuries,
\v 25 along with his relatives: Eliezer’s son Rehavyah, Rehavyah’s son Isaiah, Isaiah’s son Joram, Joram’s son Zikri, and Zikri’s son Shelomot.
\v 26 Shelomot and his relatives supervised all the storehouses for the sacred things that were consecrated by King David, the clan leaders, and the army division leaders and other commanders
\v 27 who’d dedicated some of the plunder from their battles to the repair of Yahweh’s residence.
\v 28 Shelomot and his relatives were also in charge of everything that the prophet Samuel, \add King\add* Saul Kish’s son), and \add David’s two army commanders\add* Abner (Ner’s son) and Joab Tseruyah’s son) had dedicated \add to Yahweh\add*.
\s1 Other Levites’ duties
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 29 From Yitshar’s descendants, Kenanyah and his sons worked outside \add the temple area\add* as officials and judges over Israel.
\p
\v 30 From Hebron’s descendants, Hashavyah and his relatives were responsible for all the work done for Yahweh and for the king in the area west of the Jordan \add river\add*. There were 1,700 of them who were able to do their work well.
\v 31 According to the records of Hebron’s descendants, Yeriyah was their leader. (In the fortieth year of David’s reign, they’d searched \add in those records\add* and discovered that there were powerful men in Yazer in Gilead.)
\v 32 Yeriyah had 2,700 relatives who were strong \add workers\add* and leaders of their families. King David put them in charge of governing the Reuben and Gad tribes, and the eastern half of the Manasseh tribe, to ensure that all the people did what God commanded and what the king told them to do.
\c 27
\s1 King David’s officials
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 This is a list of the Israelite men who served the king \add in the army\add*. Some were leaders of families, some were commanders of divisions of a thousand, some were commanders of divisions of a hundred, and some were other officers. There were twenty-four thousand men in each group, and each group served one month each year.
\li1
\v 2 Zavdiyel’s son Yashaveam was in charge of the group that served during the first month each year.
\v 3 \add He\add* was a descendant of Perez, and was the commander of all the army officers during the first month \add each year\add*.
\li1
\v 4 Doday (the Ahohi) was the commander of the group that served during the second month each year. Miklot was \add also\add* a leader in that group.
\li1
\v 5 Benayah, son of Yehoyada the high priest, was the commander of the group that served during the third month each year.
\v 6 He was the strongest of ‘the thirty’ and was their leader. His son was Amizavad.
\li1
\v 7 The fourth leader, for the fourth month, was Joab’s brother Asahel, and then his son Zevadyah succeeded him. His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 8 The fifth leader, for the fifth month, was the commander Shamhut the Yizrah. His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 9 The sixth leader, for the sixth month, was Ikesh’s son Ira the Tekoei. His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 10 The seventh leader, for the seventh month, was Helets the Peloni (an Efrati). His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 11 The eighth leader, for the eighth month, was Sibkay the Hushati (a Zarhi). His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 12 The ninth leader, for the ninth month, was Aviezer the Antoti (a Benyamite). His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 13 The tenth leader, for the tenth month, was Maharay the Netofati (a Zarhi). His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 14 The eleventh leader, for the eleventh month, was Benayah the Pireatoni (an Efrati). His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\li1
\v 15 The twelfth leader, for the twelfth month, was Helday the Otnieli (a Netofati). His division was twenty-four thousand men.
\s1 The teachers of Israel’s descendants
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 16 There were certain tribal leaders over each of Israel’s tribes:
\li1 Zikri’s son Eliezer was \add the leader of the\add* Reuben \add tribe\add*.
\li1 Ma’akah’s son Shefatyah was the leader of the Simeon \add tribe\add*.
\li1
\v 17 Kemuel’s son Hashavyah was the leader of the Levites.
\li1 Zadok was the leader of Aaron’s descendants.
\li1
\v 18 David’s \add older\add* brother Elihu was the leader of the Judah \add tribe\add*.
\li1 Michael’s son Omri was the leader of the Issachar \add tribe\add*.
\li1
\v 19 Obadiah’s son Yishmaeyah was the leader of the Zebulun \add tribe\add*.
\li1 Azriel’s son Yerimot was the leader of the Naphtali \add tribe\add*.
\li1
\v 20 Azazyah’s son Hoshea was the leader of the Ephraim \add tribe\add*.
\li1 Pedayah’s son Joel was the leader of the Manasseh half-tribe.
\li1
\v 21 Zechariah’s son Yido was the leader of the Manasseh half-tribe in Gilead.
\li1 Abner’s son Ya’asiyel was the leader of the Benjamin \add tribe\add*.
\li1
\v 22 Yeroham’s son Azarel was the leader of the Dan \add tribe\add*.
\m Those were the commanders of Israel’s tribes.
\p
\v 23 David hadn’t included men who were less than twenty years old when he’d ordered a census, because Yahweh had promised that he would multiply the Israelites like stars in the sky.
\v 24 Joab Tseruyah’s son) began the count, but never finished it, because Yahweh’s anger had struck Israel, so the census result wasn’t recorded in the record of King David’s reign.
\s1 King David’s administrators
\s4 Full kingdom
\li1
\v 25 Adiel’s son Azmavet supervised the king’s treasuries.
\li1 Uzziah’s son Jonathan was in charge of the storehouses in the countryside and in the cities and villages, and in the watchtowers.
\li1
\v 26 Keluv’s son Ezri supervised the farm workers.
\li1
\v 27 Shimei the Ramati supervised the \add king’s\add* vineyards.
\li1 Zavdi the Shifmi supervised the wine vats.
\li1
\v 28 Baal-Hanan the Gederi supervised the olive groves and the sycamore trees in the lowlands.
\li1 Yoash supervised the \add olive\add* oil storage.
\li1
\v 29 Shitray from the Sharon plain was in charge of the herds \add of cattle\add* that grazed there.
\li1 Adlay’s son Shafat was in charge of the cattle in the valleys.
\li1
\v 30 Ovil the Ishmaelite was in charge of the camels.
\li1 Yehdiyah the Meronoti was in charge of the donkeys.
\li1
\v 31 Yaziz the Hagri was in charge of the flocks \add of sheep and goats\add*.
\m All of those officials supervised King David’s property.
\s1 King David’s advisers
\s4 Full kingdom
\li1
\v 32 David’s uncle Jonathan was a wise adviser and a copyist.
\li1 Hakmoni’s son Yehiel attended to the king’s sons.
\li1
\v 33 Ahitofel was the king’s adviser.
\li1 Hushay the Arki was the king’s friend.
\li1
\v 34 Ahitofel was succeeded by Benayah’s son Yehoyada, then by Abiathar.
\li1 Joab was the commander of the king’s army.
\c 28
\s1 David’s temple plans
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now David called all of Israel’s leaders to assemble in Jerusalem: the tribal leaders, the leaders of the \add various\add* divisions that served the king, the commanders of army units of thousands and of hundreds, and the supervisors of all the property and cattle belonging to the king and his sons, along with the top officials, and the warriors, including their powerful heroes.
\p
\v 2 Then King David stood up and spoke, “Listen to me, my brothers and my people, I wanted in my heart to put up a building for the box containing Yahweh’s agreement to rest in. It’s the footstool for our god’s feet, and I’ve made preparations to start building,
\v 3 but God told me that I wouldn’t be the one to build a house to honour him, because I’ve been involved in a lot of war and killings.
\p
\v 4 “Yet Israel’s god Yahweh chose me from all of my father’s family to be king over Israel forever, because he chose a leader from the Judah \add tribe\add*, my father’s tribe, and among my father’s sons, he was pleased to make me king over all of Israel.
\v 5 Then out of the many sons that he’s given me, he chose my son Solomon to be the next king to rule his kingdom of Israel.
\p
\v 6 “Then he told me, ‘Your son Solomon is the one who’ll build my temple and the courtyards \add around it\add*, because I’ve chosen him to be like my son, and I’ll be like a father to him.
\v 7 I’ll establish his kingdom forever, if he’s diligent at following my instructions and \add obeying\add* my judgements, like he is at present.’
\p
\v 8 “So now, in front of all of Israel, Yahweh’s assembly, and in the hearing of our god, \add I challenge you all to discover and obey\add* all of your god Yahweh’s commands, so that you’ll all possess the good land, and you’ll leave your sons after you to inherit it forever.
\p
\v 9 “And you, Solomon my son, follow your father’s god and serve him with a whole heart and with a willing life, because Yahweh looks into all hearts, and understands all your motives. If you look for him, he’ll \add make sure\add* you find him, but if you abandon him, he’ll reject you forever.
\v 10 You can now see that you need to be strong and get busy, because Yahweh has chosen you to erect a building to be his sanctuary.”
\p
\v 11 Then David gave the plans for \add the temple\add* to his son Solomon, including plans for its porch, its houses, its treasuries, its upper rooms and its inner rooms, and the room to contain the sacred box.
\v 12 \add He also handed over\add* the plans he envisioned for the temple courtyards, all the surrounding apartments, all the treasuries, and all the storerooms for sacred things.
\v 13 \add He included instructions\add* for the divisions of the priests and Levites, for all the services required to operate the temple, and for all the equipment and utensils needed for all that.
\v 14 \add David had determined\add* the weight of gold and silver that would be needed to make all the necessary items that would be used in the regular \add temple\add* functions,
\v 15 \add including\add* the gold lampstands and lamps and the silver lampstands and lamps,
\v 16 and the gold for the display tables, and the silver for the silver tables.
\v 17 Also the pure gold meat forks and the jugs and basins, and the weights of the gold and silver bowls.
\v 18 Then the weight of refined gold for the incense altar, and for making the winged creatures that spread out across the top of the box containing Yahweh’s agreement.
\v 19 \add He said\add*, “I’ve written down all those plans following Yahweh’s direction, as he gave me instructions for what has to be done.”
\v 20 Then David told his son Solomon, “Be strong and courageous, and get going. Don’t be afraid or discouraged, because Yahweh God, my god, is with you. He won’t leave you and he won’t abandon you before all the work of constructing Yahweh’s residence and the necessary equipment is finished.
\v 21 Listen, the divisions of the priests and the Levites for all the work in God’s house, and all the skilled workmen, will be backing you in all the work. The officials and all the people are ready to follow your instructions.”
\c 29
\s1 Donations for the temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then King David told the entire assembly, “My son Solomon has been chosen by God himself. He’s young and tender, and it’s a huge job, because the temple isn’t being built for people, but for Yahweh God.
\v 2 I’ve used all my resources to provide a plentiful supply of materials for my god’s residence: the gold for the gold things, and the silver for the silver things, the bronze for the bronze things, the iron for the iron things, the wood for the wooden parts, onyx stones for inlays, antimony stones and colourful things, and all the rare stones, and alabaster stones.
\v 3 What’s more, because I’m excited to have my god’s residence \add built\add*, I’ve donated my own personal gold and silver on top of everything else that I’ve prepared for the sacred temple:
\v 4 100 tonnes of gold and 2,500 tonnes of refined silver for overlaying the buildings’ walls,
\v 5 \add as well as\add* gold for the gold things, silver for the silver things, and for everything the craftsmen are going to make. So who’s willing to commit themselves today to making a contribution towards Yahweh’s work?
\p
\v 6 Then the family leaders and the leaders of the tribes, and the various levels of military commanders, and the king’s construction overseers, \add all\add* contributed willingly.
\v 7 They donated \add a total of\add* 170 tonnes of gold and 100 kilograms of gold coins, 350 tonnes of silver, 600 tonnes of bronze, and 3,400 tonnes of iron for the temple construction,
\v 8 plus anyone who had precious stones donated them to the treasury for the temple. Yehiel (a Gershoni) was in charge of those.
\v 9 The people were excited, because they’d \add all\add* given willingly to Yahweh, and King David was also very pleased.
\s1 The praying for David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then \add King\add* David blessed Yahweh in front of the entire assembly, saying, “You are blessed, Yahweh, Israel’s god, our father, for all time, both past and future.
\v 11 Greatness, power, beauty, splendour, and majesty \add all belong\add* to you, Yahweh, as indeed does everything in the heavens and on the earth. The kingdom and supreme leadership \add all belong\add* to you, Yahweh.
\v 12 Wealth and \add prestige comes from you\add*, and you rule over everything. You have the power and strength and control to make \add people\add* great and to strengthen everyone,
\v 13 so now, our god, we’re thanking you and praising your beautiful name.
\p
\v 14 But who am I, and who are my people, that we should be in a position to be able to donate freely like this? Yes, everything comes from you, and we’ve simply given back from what you gave to us,
\v 15 because we’re \add just\add* strangers on the earth in your eyes, and foreign guests, like all our ancestors. Our time on the earth is like a shadow without any permanence.
\v 16 Yahweh our god, all this abundance that we’ve collected in order to build a house for you, for your holy name, originally came from you, and it all belongs to you \add anyway\add*.
\v 17 Yes I know, my god, that you examine our hearts, and you’re pleased when we do what’s right. I’ve freely offered all these \add gifts\add* as I strive to do what’s right, and now I’m also happy to see your people freely offer \add gifts\add* to you.
\v 18 Yahweh, god of our ancestors Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, \add help your people continue to desire to do things like this forever, and help them remain loyal to you\add*.
\v 19 Also, give my son Solomon a sincere desire to follow your instructions, your decrees, and your regulations, and to do everything needed to build the temple following all my preparation work.”
\p
\v 20 Then David told all the assembled \add people\add*, “Bless your god Yahweh,” and the entire assembly blessed Yahweh, their ancestors’ god. Then they bowed down to honour \add both\add* Yahweh and the king.
\p
\v 21 Then they offered sacrifices to Yahweh, and sacrificed burnt offerings to Yahweh on the following day: 1,000 bulls, 1,000 rams, 1,000 lambs, plus their drink offerings and sacrifices for all of Israel in abundance.
\v 22 They ate and drank in Yahweh’s honour that day with great celebration.
\p Then they rededicated David’s son Solomon as king, and anointed him as leader on Yahweh’s behalf, and Zadok as priest.
\v 23 Solomon sat on Yahweh’s throne as king, replacing his father David, and he prospered, and all of Israel listened to him.
\v 24 All the leaders and the mighty warriors, as well as all of King David’s sons, pledged allegiance to King Solomon.
\v 25 Yahweh made Solomon exceedingly great in the eyes of all the Israelites, and bestowed a kingdom on him, the majesty of which hadn’t previously been on any king over Israel.
\s1 The story of David’s kingdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 26 Jesse’s son David was the king who ruled all of Israel,
\v 27 and he ruled for forty years: from Hebron \add city\add* for seven years, and then thirty-three years from Jerusalem.
\v 28 He lived a long life with riches and splendour, and died at a good old age. Then his son Solomon replaced him as king.
\v 29 \add The records\add* of everything King David did, from the beginning to his end, were written by the prophets Samuel, Nathan, and Gad.
\v 30 They wrote about his powerful rule, and all the things that happened to him and to the Israelite people, and in the kingdoms of other countries while he was ruling Israel.
